// Dibangun perkakas/bangun_situs.py.
window.JRG_CARI = [{"j": "JawaRustGo++", "u": "web_dokumentasi/index.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "A programming language with four execution engines, diagnostics that explain the way out, and a library big enough for real applications.", "t": "JawaRustGo++, JRG for short, is a programming language written in Rust. Its code looks like Rust and Go, it is dynamically typed, and its messages explain what went wrong and where. These docs are for version **0.6.0**. pub fn main() { let name = \"Nusantara\" wok(\"\"\"welcome, ${name}!\"\"\") } `wok` prints to the screen. The **Run** button above a code block runs the example right in the browser, and **Edit** makes it editable. What is inside **Four engines.** The interpreter (the default), a bytecode VM (`--vm`), a JIT that compiles hot code while it runs (`--jit`), and native compilation through LLVM (`--emit-llvm`). The same program prints the same output on all four. A fifth engine, WebAssembly, is being built so JRG programs run on the web. **Values never share.** Copying, passing, and returning a value always yields a copy, so there is no garbage collector and no surprising change from a distance. **Errors you can read.** Every error names the file, line, column, the piece of code, and usually the way out. Messages are in Indonesian today; English messages are being written. **A complete library.** 871 built-in functions and the `std/` modules: text, collections, files, JSON, HTTP, a web server, SQLite, canvas, sound, numerics, and a 2D game engine. **Other languages.** Call C, C++, Rust, Go, Python, JavaScript, PHP, Lua, Java, Kotlin, .NET, and WebAssembly. **Tools.** `jrgm` for projects, dependencies, tests, formatting, and linting; a terminal editor; a REPL; a langu"}, {"j": "What is inside", "u": "web_dokumentasi/index.html#what-is-inside", "g": "Introduction", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Where to start", "u": "web_dokumentasi/index.html#where-to-start", "g": "Introduction", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Status", "u": "web_dokumentasi/index.html#status", "g": "Introduction", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Install", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pasang.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Use a ready-made binary, or build from source. Either way you get two programs, jrgc and jrgm.", "t": "Once installed you have two commands: `jrgc` runs, checks, and compiles `.jrg` files. Without arguments it opens the REPL. `jrgm` manages projects: creating, running, testing, handling dependencies, all the way to packaging an APK. Ready-made binaries Releases for Linux (x86_64, aarch64), macOS (Intel, Apple Silicon), Windows, and Termux are on the [Releases](https://github.com/Kodok-Kejepit/jrg/releases) page. On Linux, macOS, and Termux, the installer downloads the matching file, checks its checksum, and puts `jrgc` and `jrgm` in `~/.local/bin` (on Termux: `$PREFIX/bin`): curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Kodok-Kejepit/jrg/main/rilis/pasang.sh | sh # a specific version, or another folder sh pasang.sh --versi 0.6.0 --ke /usr/local/bin Make sure `~/.local/bin` is on your `PATH`, then check: jrgc --version From source All you need is stable [Rust](https://rustup.rs). git clone https://github.com/Kodok-Kejepit/jrg cd jrg cargo build --release ./target/release/jrgc --version # install into ~/.cargo/bin cargo install --path . The first build takes a few minutes and about 2 GB of memory. On a small machine, limit the number of jobs: `cargo build --release -j 1`. Termux (Android) The same installer command works on Termux. It recognises Termux and uses the Android binary, because an ordinary Linux binary does not run there. To build it yourself: pkg install rust git git clone https://github.com/Kodok-Kejepit/jrg cd jrg cargo build --release -j 1 `-j 1` matters on a phone"}, {"j": "Ready-made binaries", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pasang.html#ready-made-binaries", "g": "Install", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "From source", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pasang.html#from-source", "g": "Install", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Termux (Android)", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pasang.html#termux-android", "g": "Install", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Optional extras", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pasang.html#optional-extras", "g": "Install", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Your first file", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pasang.html#your-first-file", "g": "Install", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Quick tour", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Your first ten minutes with JRG, from one line to a program with tests.", "t": "One line The shortest `.jrg` file holds just the statement: wok(\"hello\") jrgc hello.jrg A file without `package` is a *script*: top-level lines run from top to bottom. Functions, structs, and other types can be declared anywhere in the file. wok(greet(\"Ani\")) fn greet(name) { return \"hello, \" + name } let numbers = [5, 3, 8, 1] wok(\"sorted:\", arr::sort(numbers)) A program with main For bigger programs, write `package` and a `pub fn main()`. That is where the program starts. package main fn to_fahrenheit(c: float) -> float { return c * 9.0 / 5.0 + 32.0 } pub fn main() { for _, c in [0.0, 25.0, 100.0] { wok(c, \"°C =\", to_fahrenheit(c), \"°F\") } } `wok` takes any number of arguments and separates them with spaces. Its siblings: `wok_` (no newline), `eprint` (to stderr), and `str::format` for building text. Reading input and arguments `tok()` reads one line from the keyboard. `env::args()` holds the script name, then the arguments written after `--`. let name = tok() wok(\"hello,\", name) wok(env::args()) $ jrgc ask.jrg -- one two Ani hello, Ani [ask.jrg, one, two] The other engines The same program can run in several ways: jrgc temperature.jrg # interpreter jrgc temperature.jrg --vm # bytecode VM, faster jrgc temperature.jrg --jit # VM plus machine code for the hot parts jrgc temperature.jrg --emit-llvm # native compilation through clang, then run jrgc temperature.jrg --emit-llvm -o temperature --no-run # compile only; the result is ./temperature The differences are explained in [T"}, {"j": "One line", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#one-line", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "A program with main", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#a-program-with-main", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Reading input and arguments", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#reading-input-and-arguments", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "The other engines", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#the-other-engines", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Trying things in the REPL", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#trying-things-in-the-repl", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Writing tests", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#writing-tests", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "When something goes wrong", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#when-something-goes-wrong", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Next", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tur.html#next", "g": "Quick tour", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Values and types", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Numbers, text, arrays, tuples, dicts, and how to bind them to names.", "t": "Binding names Form Can be reassigned? --- --- `let x = 1` no `const X = 1` no `var x = 1` yes `x := 1` yes, and it declares the name `let mut x = 1` yes, same as `var` var score = 10 score = score + 5 x := 3 x = x * 2 const LIMIT = 100 wok(score, x, LIMIT) A name must be declared before it is assigned. `totl = 5` on a name that does not exist is refused, so a typo never quietly creates a new variable. A name bound with `let` cannot have its contents changed either: `let a = [1, 2]` followed by `a[0] = 9` is refused. Type annotations may be written (`let n: int = 5`), and JRG checks that the type name exists. The type itself is not enforced: JRG is dynamically typed, so annotations work as documentation. Numbers `int` is a 128-bit integer, `float` a 64-bit floating-point number. Dividing two `int`s gives an `int` (truncated toward zero), and `%` is the remainder. wok(7 / 2, 7 % 2, 7.0 / 2.0, -7 / 2) wok(2 ** 10, 2.0 ** 0.5) wok(1_000_000, 0xff, 0b1010) wok(0.1 + 0.2) Overflow never wraps silently. `170141183460469231731687303715884105727 + 1` is an error, and so is division by zero. For arithmetic that really should wrap (hashes, random generators), use `math::wrap_add`, `math::wrap_sub`, and `math::wrap_mul`. Fixed-width integer types exist too: `i8` to `i128`, `u8` to `u128`, `isize`, `usize`, and `byte` (a number 0–255). Floating point: `f32`, `f64`. Text `string` is UTF-8 text. Indices count characters, not bytes, and a single character has type `rune` (written `'a'`). let"}, {"j": "Binding names", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#binding-names", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Numbers", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#numbers", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Text", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#text", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Arrays", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#arrays", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Tuples", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#tuples", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Dicts", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#dicts", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Conversions", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#conversions", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Empty values", "u": "web_dokumentasi/nilai.html#empty-values", "g": "Values and types", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Control flow", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Branches, loops, and how to leave both.", "t": "if and else The condition has no parentheses, and the body always has braces. `if` can also be used as a value. let n = 7 if n % 2 == 0 { wok(\"even\") } else if n > 5 { wok(\"odd and big\") } else { wok(\"odd\") } let size = if n > 5 { \"big\" } else { \"small\" } wok(size) Ternary The short form is `condition $ if_true : if_false`. It opens with `$`, not `?`, because `?` is used to propagate errors (see [Option and Result](galat.html)). let score = 72 wok(score >= 75 $ \"pass\" : \"retake\") for `for` has two forms, and the difference matters: `for i, x in collection`: `i` is the index, `x` the value. Use `_` for the one you do not need. `for i in collection`: **a single name always means the index**, ranges included. let fruit = [\"apple\", \"orange\"] for i, f in fruit { wok(i, f) } var values = [] for _, number in 5..8 { values = values.push(number) } wok(values) var indices = [] for i in 5..8 { indices = indices.push(i) } wok(indices) The last line holds `[0, 1, 2]`, not `[5, 6, 7]`: with one name, `i` is the 0th, 1st, and 2nd index of that range. If you want the values, write `for _, i in 5..8`. `a..b` excludes `b`, `a..=b` includes it. The two-name form also works on text (index and character) and dicts (key and value). A C-style form exists too: for i := 0; i 50 { break } } wok(guess) Labeled break and continue `break` and `continue` apply to the nearest loop. To leave an outer loop, give it a label that starts with a single quote. 'search: for _, i in 1..10 { for _, j in 1..10 { if i"}, {"j": "if and else", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html#if-and-else", "g": "Control flow", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Ternary", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html#ternary", "g": "Control flow", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "for", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html#for", "g": "Control flow", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "while and loop", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html#while-and-loop", "g": "Control flow", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Labeled break and continue", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html#labeled-break-and-continue", "g": "Control flow", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "match", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html#match", "g": "Control flow", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "defer", "u": "web_dokumentasi/kendali.html#defer", "g": "Control flow", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Functions and closures", "u": "web_dokumentasi/fungsi.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Declaring functions, flexible parameters, functions as values, and closures.", "t": "Declaring functions fn area(w: int, h: int) -> int { return w * h } fn perimeter(w, h) { return 2 * (w + h) } fn double(x) = x * 2 wok(area(3, 4), perimeter(3, 4), double(5)) Type annotations on parameters and the return value may be written or left out. `fn f(x) = expression` is the short form for a function that only returns one expression. A function may be called before its line, and a function may be declared inside another function (but it does not see the outer function's locals; use a closure for that). Parameters fn greet(name, greeting = \"hello\") { return greeting + \", \" + name } fn total(numbers...) { return arr::sum(numbers) } fn divide(numerator, denominator) { return numerator / denominator } wok(greet(\"Ani\"), greet(\"Budi\", \"good morning\")) wok(total(), total(1, 2, 3)) wok(total(...[4, 5, 6])) wok(divide(denominator: 2, numerator: 10)) **Defaults** (`greeting = \"hello\"`) are evaluated at call time, and may use earlier parameters. **A collector** (`numbers...`) takes the remaining arguments as an array; with none left it is `[]`. **Spread** (`...arr`) is the reverse: the array's elements become separate arguments. **Named arguments** (`denominator: 2`) may come in any order and mix with positional ones. A parameter may be a pattern: `fn distance((x1, y1), (x2, y2))` unpacks two tuples right in the function head. Returning several values Return a tuple, then unpack it at the call site: fn stats(xs) { return (arr::min(xs), arr::max(xs), arr::sum(xs) / len(xs)) } lo"}, {"j": "Declaring functions", "u": "web_dokumentasi/fungsi.html#declaring-functions", "g": "Functions and closures", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Parameters", "u": "web_dokumentasi/fungsi.html#parameters", "g": "Functions and closures", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Returning several values", "u": "web_dokumentasi/fungsi.html#returning-several-values", "g": "Functions and closures", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Closure", "u": "web_dokumentasi/fungsi.html#closure", "g": "Functions and closures", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Functions as values", "u": "web_dokumentasi/fungsi.html#functions-as-values", "g": "Functions and closures", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Recursion", "u": "web_dokumentasi/fungsi.html#recursion", "g": "Functions and closures", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Structs, enums, and traits", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tipe.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Making your own types, giving them methods, and sharing behaviour through traits.", "t": "Struct A struct groups named data. Fields marked `pub` can be read from outside; the rest are only visible from that type's methods (and those of its descendants). A field may have a default, and a field without one must be filled in. struct Account { pub name: string, pub balance: int = 0, pin: string = \"0000\", } impl Account { fn new(name) -> Account { return Account { name: name } } fn deposit(self, n) -> Account { var a = self a.balance += n return a } fn check_pin(self, p) -> bool { return self.pin == p } } var a = Account::new(\"Ani\") a = a.deposit(50) wok(a.name, a.balance, a.check_pin(\"1234\")) A function in an `impl` without `self` is called through the type name (`Account::new`). One that takes `self` is called through a value (`a.deposit(50)`). Inside a method, `self` is a **copy**. Changing `self` does not change the caller's value, so a method that changes something returns a new value and the caller reassigns it: `a = a.deposit(50)`. That form does not copy large data; see [Memory model](memori.html). Building from another value `...` fills the fields that are not written from another value. It must come last. struct Point { pub x: int, pub y: int, pub z: int } let start = Point { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 } let moved = Point { x: 10, ...start } wok(moved) Inheritance A struct may inherit from one other struct. The parent's fields and methods come along. struct Animal { pub name: string } impl Animal { fn intro(self) -> string { return \"I am \" + self.name } } struct Cat: A"}, {"j": "Struct", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tipe.html#struct", "g": "Structs, enums, traits", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Enum", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tipe.html#enum", "g": "Structs, enums, traits", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Trait", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tipe.html#trait", "g": "Structs, enums, traits", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Generics", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tipe.html#generics", "g": "Structs, enums, traits", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Your own operators and display", "u": "web_dokumentasi/tipe.html#your-own-operators-and-display", "g": "Structs, enums, traits", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Patterns and match", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pola.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Taking values apart and choosing a branch by their shape.", "t": "match `match` tries each arm from the top and runs the first one that matches. `_` matches anything. fn describe(x) { return match x { 0 => \"zero\", n @ 1..=9 => \"one digit: \" + str(n), n if n \"negative\", _ => \"big\", } } wok(describe(0), describe(7), describe(-3), describe(42)) Pattern Matches --- --- `0`, `\"teks\"`, `'a'`, `true` exactly that value `1..=9`, `'a'..='z'` a value inside the range `a | b` either of them `n` anything, binding it to `n` `n @ pattern` `pattern`, binding the whole value to `n` `pattern if condition` `pattern`, when `condition` is true `(a, b)` a two-element tuple `[a, b]`, `[]`, `[head, ...rest]`, `[a, ..., z]` an array of that shape `Point { x, y: 0 }` a struct whose field `y` is 0 `Shape::Circle(r)`, `Some(v)`, `Err(e)` an enum variant, unpacking its contents Array patterns fn shape(a) { return match a { [] => \"empty\", [x] => \"one: \" + str(x), [first, ..., last] => str(first) + \" to \" + str(last), } } wok(shape([])) wok(shape([5])) wok(shape([1, 2, 3, 4])) Patterns in let and for Patterns also work in `let`, in a `for` header, and in function parameters. let (a, b) = (1, 2) let [p, q, ...rest] = [10, 20, 30, 40] wok(a, b, p, q, rest) let scores = [(\"Ani\", 90), (\"Budi\", 75)] for _, (name, score) in scores { wok(name, score >= 80 $ \"A\" : \"B\") } struct Point { pub x: int, pub y: int } let Point { x, y } = Point { x: 3, y: 4 } wok(x * y) let … else When the pattern might not match, `let pattern = value else { … }` runs the `else` block. That block must "}, {"j": "match", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pola.html#match", "g": "Patterns", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Array patterns", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pola.html#array-patterns", "g": "Patterns", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Patterns in let and for", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pola.html#patterns-in-let-and-for", "g": "Patterns", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "let … else", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pola.html#let-else", "g": "Patterns", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "??", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pola.html#bagian", "g": "Patterns", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Bare variants", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pola.html#bare-variants", "g": "Patterns", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Option, Result, and errors", "u": "web_dokumentasi/galat.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "How JRG says \"might not be there\" and \"might fail\", and how to stop a program on purpose.", "t": "Option `Option` is an answer that may be empty: `Some(value)` or `None`. A built-in function that may find nothing (`first`, `get`, `find`, `pop`, `dict::get`, `env::get`, `str::find`) returns an `Option`, never `nil` or `-1`. let found = [4, 8, 15].find(|x| x > 5) let empty = [4, 8, 15].find(|x| x > 50) wok(found, empty) wok(found.is_some(), empty.is_none()) wok(found.unwrap(), empty.unwrap_or(0), empty ?? -1) wok(found.map(|x| x * 2), empty.map(|x| x * 2)) `unwrap()` on `None` is an error. Use `unwrap_or`, `??`, or `match` when emptiness is expected. Result `Result` is an answer that may fail: `Ok(value)` or `Err(reason)`. Functions that touch the outside world (`fs::try_read`, `http::get`, FFI, databases) return a `Result`, and your own functions may too. fn divide(a, b) -> Result { if b == 0 { return Err(\"division by zero\") } return Ok(a / b) } wok(divide(10, 2), divide(1, 0)) match divide(9, 0) { Ok(v) => wok(\"result\", v), Err(e) => wok(\"failed:\", e), } wok(divide(8, 2).unwrap_or(-1), divide(8, 0).unwrap_or(-1)) Propagating failure with ? `expression?` opens an `Ok`, or returns its `Err` straight out of the running function. It works on `Option` too: a `None` is passed on. fn divide(a, b) -> Result { if b == 0 { return Err(\"division by zero\") } return Ok(a / b) } fn compute(a, b, c) -> Result { let x = divide(a, b)? let y = divide(x, c)? return Ok(y) } wok(compute(100, 5, 2)) wok(compute(100, 0, 2)) When main fails `main` may return a `Result`. An `Err` that leaves `main"}, {"j": "Option", "u": "web_dokumentasi/galat.html#option", "g": "Option and Result", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Result", "u": "web_dokumentasi/galat.html#result", "g": "Option and Result", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Propagating failure with ?", "u": "web_dokumentasi/galat.html#propagating-failure-with", "g": "Option and Result", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "When main fails", "u": "web_dokumentasi/galat.html#when-main-fails", "g": "Option and Result", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Stopping a program", "u": "web_dokumentasi/galat.html#stopping-a-program", "g": "Option and Result", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Checking assumptions", "u": "web_dokumentasi/galat.html#checking-assumptions", "g": "Option and Result", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Modules and import", "u": "web_dokumentasi/modul.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Splitting a program into several files, and using the std/ library.", "t": "One file, one module Every `.jrg` file is a module. Another file uses it through `import`, and calls what is inside with the `name::` prefix. package geometry pub const PI = 3.14159 pub fn circle_area(r: float) -> float { return PI * r * r } fn _helper() { return 1 } pub struct Point { pub x: float, pub y: float } impl Point { pub fn distance(self, other: Point) -> float { return math::sqrt((self.x - other.x) ** 2.0 + (self.y - other.y) ** 2.0) } } package main import \"geometry\" import \"geometry\" as g pub fn main() { wok(geometry::circle_area(2.0)) let a = g::Point { x: 0.0, y: 0.0 } let b = geometry::Point { x: 3.0, y: 4.0 } wok(a.distance(b), g::PI) } $ jrgc main.jrg 12.56636 5.0 3.14159 What is visible from outside What an importer can use: functions, top-level `const`/`let`, structs, enums with their variants, and the methods of types the module owns. A name starting with `_` is private. `geometry::_helper()` is refused with a message that says why. A top-level `var` stays with its module. If its value has to be read from outside, provide a reader function. A file only sees the modules it imports itself. Modules imported by another module are not visible. A file is one module however many times it is imported, under whatever alias. Where import looks `import \"geometry\"` looks for `geometry.jrg` in the importing file's folder, then in the project's dependency folders (see [jrgm](jrgm.html)), then in folders added with `jrgc -I folder`. Nested paths work too: `import \"tools"}, {"j": "One file, one module", "u": "web_dokumentasi/modul.html#one-file-one-module", "g": "Modules", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "What is visible from outside", "u": "web_dokumentasi/modul.html#what-is-visible-from-outside", "g": "Modules", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Where import looks", "u": "web_dokumentasi/modul.html#where-import-looks", "g": "Modules", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "The std/ library", "u": "web_dokumentasi/modul.html#the-std-library", "g": "Modules", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Shadowing built-in names", "u": "web_dokumentasi/modul.html#shadowing-built-in-names", "g": "Modules", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Concurrency", "u": "web_dokumentasi/konkuren.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Goroutines, channels, select, Futures, and synchronisation tools.", "t": "JRG's concurrency model is like Go's: do work in a goroutine, then communicate through channels. Each goroutine is a real operating-system thread, so it suits tens to hundreds of tasks, not millions. spawn and channels `spawn { … }` (or `go f(x)`) runs a block in a new goroutine. `make_chan(type, capacity)` makes a channel; `c <- v` sends, `<-c` receives. fn heavy(n) { return n * n } let c = make_chan(int, 2) spawn { c <- heavy(3) } spawn { c <- heavy(4) } wok(<-c + <-c) Variables used inside `spawn` are copied when the goroutine is created. The program waits for every goroutine to finish before it ends, so no output is lost because `main` finished first. select `select` waits on several channels at once and takes the first one ready. A `default` arm makes it not wait. let results = make_chan(string, 0) let done = make_chan(bool, 0) spawn { for _, word in [\"one\", \"two\", \"three\"] { results <- word } done <- true } var running = true while running { select { case w := <-results: { wok(\"received\", w) } case _ := <-done: { running = false } } } Future and Task For \"start now, collect the result later\", use `Future::new(function, arguments…)` and then `future::await`. fn sum_to(n) { var t = 0 for _, i in 0..n { t += i } return t } let a = Future::new(sum_to, 1000) let b = Future::new(sum_to, 2000) wok(future::await(a) + future::await(b)) A Future's result can only be collected once; collecting it again is an error. Others in the family: `future::all`, `future::race`, `future::time"}, {"j": "spawn and channels", "u": "web_dokumentasi/konkuren.html#spawn-and-channels", "g": "Concurrency", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "select", "u": "web_dokumentasi/konkuren.html#select", "g": "Concurrency", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Future and Task", "u": "web_dokumentasi/konkuren.html#future-and-task", "g": "Concurrency", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Atomic and Mutex", "u": "web_dokumentasi/konkuren.html#atomic-and-mutex", "g": "Concurrency", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "What is not there", "u": "web_dokumentasi/konkuren.html#what-is-not-there", "g": "Concurrency", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Built-in library", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pustaka.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "871 built-in functions in namespaces, plus the std/ modules written in JRG.", "t": "Finding a function No need to memorise. From the terminal: $ jrgc --cari sort arr::sort arr::sort_by arr::sort_by_key arr::sort_desc heap::to_sorted_array wokwok::argsort wokwok::sort $ jrgc --doc arr::sort_by_key arr::sort_by_key(a, f) -> array Salinan terurut menaik menurut `f(x)`; `f` dipanggil sekali per elemen. In the REPL the commands are `:cari` and `:doc`. A mistyped function name is answered with a suggestion (\"did you mean `upper`?\"). Most `arr::` and `str::` functions can also be called as methods: `arr::sort(a)` is the same as `a.sort()`. Everyday examples wok(math::sqrt(16.0), math::gcd(12, 18), math::round(2.567)) wok(arr::unique([1, 1, 2]), arr::chunks([1, 2, 3, 4, 5], 2)) wok(str::title(\"ada lovelace\"), str::words(\"one two three\")) let j = json::parse(\"{\\\"name\\\": \\\"Ani\\\", \\\"scores\\\": [90, 85]}\") wok(j[\"name\"], j[\"scores\"][1], json::stringify({\"ok\": true})) wok(re::test(\"[0-9]+\", \"code 4521\"), re::replace_all(\"[0-9]\", \"x9y8\", \"#\")) fs::write(\"notes.txt\", \"line one\\nline two\\n\") wok(fs::read_lines(\"notes.txt\"), fs::size(\"notes.txt\")) fs::delete(\"notes.txt\") wok(crypto::sha256(\"hello\")[0:16], crypto::base64_encode(\"JRG\")) wok(time::format(0, \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\")) Namespace Namespace Contents --- --- `str::` (52) text: slice, search, replace, split, format, change case, pad left/right `arr::` (65) arrays: sort, filter, map, fold, group, chunk, zip `dict::` (14) dicts: get, set, merge, keys, values `math::` (41) trigonometry, roots, powers, rounding, GCD/LCM, factoria"}, {"j": "Finding a function", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pustaka.html#finding-a-function", "g": "Built-in library", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Everyday examples", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pustaka.html#everyday-examples", "g": "Built-in library", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Namespace", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pustaka.html#namespace", "g": "Built-in library", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "The std/ modules", "u": "web_dokumentasi/pustaka.html#modul-std", "g": "Built-in library", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Web and data", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "An HTTP server, templates, cookies and sessions, WebSocket, an HTTP client, and SQLite.", "t": "Everything a web application needs is in the `dok::` and `http::` namespaces, with no extra library. The server package main pub fn main() { let r = dok::router_new() dok::router_get(r, \"/\", |req| dok::html(\" Hello from JRG \")) dok::router_get(r, \"/greet/:name\", |req| dok::text(\"hello, \" + req.params.name)) dok::router_get(r, \"/api/square\", |req| { let n = int(dok::query_get(req.query, \"n\") ?? \"0\") return dok::json_response({\"n\": n, \"square\": n * n}) }) dok::router_post(r, \"/echo\", |req| dok::text(req.body)) dok::router_static(r, \"/assets\", \"./public\") dok::serve(r, 8080) } $ curl localhost:8080/greet/Ani hello, Ani $ curl \"localhost:8080/api/square?n=12\" {\"n\":12,\"square\":144} `dok::serve` blocks and handles each connection in its own thread. The third argument sets the address. The default is `\"127.0.0.1\"`, so it is only reachable from the same machine (or through a proxy such as Caddy/nginx on that machine); write `\"0.0.0.0\"` to open it to the network. `dok::router_static` does not serve files or folders whose name starts with a dot (`.env`, `.git/`, `.htpasswd`): they get a 404 like a missing file, so a secret that ended up in the public folder does not leak. The exception is `.well-known/`, which is public on purpose (for example ACME certificate challenges). Files are streamed from disk. Request (`req`) Contents --- --- `method`, `path` `\"GET\"`, `\"/greet/Ani\"` `params` the `:name` parts of the route pattern, read as `req.params.name` `query` the pairs from `?a=1&b=2`; re"}, {"j": "The server", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html#the-server", "g": "Web and data", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Templates", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html#templates", "g": "Web and data", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Cookies, sessions, and passwords", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html#cookies-sessions-and-passwords", "g": "Web and data", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "WebSocket", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html#websocket", "g": "Web and data", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "The HTTP client", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html#the-http-client", "g": "Web and data", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "SQLite", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html#sqlite", "g": "Web and data", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Low-level networking", "u": "web_dokumentasi/web.html#low-level-networking", "g": "Web and data", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Canvas, sound, and numerics", "u": "web_dokumentasi/grafis.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Drawing and saving PNGs, making sound, and computing with numeric arrays.", "t": "Canvas `Canvas::new(width, height)` makes a drawing surface. Colours are written `0xRRGGBB`, and the point (0, 0) is at the top left. let c = Canvas::new(200, 120) c.clear(0xF4EFE4) c.fill_round_rect(10, 10, 180, 100, 14, 0xA94F28) c.fill_circle(60, 60, 28, 0xFFFFFF) c.draw_text(110, 52, \"JRG\", 0xFFFFFF) c.save_png(\"card.png\") let again = Canvas::load_png(\"card.png\") wok(again.width(), again.height(), again.get_pixel(60, 60)) fs::delete(\"card.png\") What is available (57 functions, all of them callable as methods too): **Shapes:** lines (plain and anti-aliased), rectangles, rounded rectangles, circles, ellipses, arcs, triangles, polygons, Bezier curves, and scaled text. **Fills:** solid colour, linear gradients, and radial gradients. **Transforms:** `translate`, `rotate`, `scale`, with a `push`/`pop` stack, and `clip`. **Images:** `save_png`, `save_bmp`, `Canvas::load_png`, and `blit`/`blit_region` to paste another canvas (sprites, tiles). **Input:** keyboard keys, mouse, and touch, for a canvas that has a window. Windows The fourth argument of `Canvas::new` chooses where it shows: Mode Result --- --- `\"polos\"` (default) no window; the image is saved to a file `\"tab\"` a desktop window (needs a `--features canvas` build) `\"web\"` shown in the browser through a local server `\"png\"` every frame is written as a PNG file The animation loop: while `c.is_open()`, draw, then call `c.update()`. `c.delta_time()` gives the length of the last frame in seconds. For games, the [game engine]("}, {"j": "Canvas", "u": "web_dokumentasi/grafis.html#canvas", "g": "Canvas, sound, numerics", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Sound", "u": "web_dokumentasi/grafis.html#sound", "g": "Canvas, sound, numerics", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Numerics: wokwok", "u": "web_dokumentasi/grafis.html#numerics-wokwok", "g": "Canvas, sound, numerics", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Game engine", "u": "web_dokumentasi/game.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "std/mesin, a 2D game engine on top of the canvas, which can be tested without opening a window.", "t": "`import \"std/mesin\"` gives a 2D game engine written in JRG. A game is three things: an initial state, an `update` function that computes the next state, and a `draw` function. The engine's own API keeps its Indonesian names (`mesin::baru`, `simulasi`, `jalankan`). The smallest game package main import \"std/mesin\" import \"std/mesin/warna\" struct Ball { pub x: float } fn update(state, input, dt) { var s = state s.x = state.x + input.arah(\"Left\", \"Right\", \"Up\", \"Down\").x * 60.0 * dt return s } fn draw(c, state) { canvas::fill_circle(c, state.x, 40, 6, warna::MERAH) } pub fn main() { let g = mesin::baru(\"Ball\", 160, 80, Ball { x: 80.0 }, update, draw) if canvas::punya_jendela() { g.jalankan() } else { wok(g.simulasi([[\"Right\"], [\"Right\"]]).keadaan.x) } } On a computer with a screen, `g.jalankan()` opens a window and the ball moves with the arrow keys. Elsewhere, `simulasi` advances the game two steps with `Right` held, then prints the position. Three rules **State is a value.** `update(state, input, dt)` returns a new state; nothing is changed in place. That is why a game can be replayed and tested. **A fixed time step**, 1/60 second by default (change it with `dengan_dt`). The physics is the same on fast and slow computers. **It runs without a window.** `simulasi(recording)` takes the list of keys held at each step. `dengan_rekam()` records a real play session, and `putar(recording)` replays it to the same state, so a bug that shows up while playing can become a test. The state "}, {"j": "The smallest game", "u": "web_dokumentasi/game.html#the-smallest-game", "g": "Game engine", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Three rules", "u": "web_dokumentasi/game.html#three-rules", "g": "Game engine", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Modules", "u": "web_dokumentasi/game.html#modules", "g": "Game engine", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "On a phone", "u": "web_dokumentasi/game.html#on-a-phone", "g": "Game engine", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Sound", "u": "web_dokumentasi/game.html#sound", "g": "Game engine", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Other languages (FFI)", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Calling C, C++, Rust, Go, Python, JavaScript, PHP, Lua, Java, Kotlin, .NET, and WebAssembly from JRG.", "t": "Every door to another language answers a `Result`: the library might not be installed, and that is an ordinary failure to handle. C, Rust, and Go Declare the signature in an `extern \"C\"` block, then call it like an ordinary function. The library name may be given. extern \"C\" { fn abs(n: i32) -> i32 fn strlen(s: string) -> usize } wok(abs(-17), strlen(\"hello\")) extern \"C\" \"libm.so.6\" { fn pow(base: f64, exponent: f64) -> f64 } extern \"C\" \"./libcalc.so\" { fn process(data: *mut i32, n: i32) -> i32 } Rust (`#[no_mangle] pub extern \"C\" fn`) and Go (`-buildmode=c-shared` with `//export`) produce plain C symbols, so they are called the same way. C++ `extern \"C++\"` computes the C++ symbol name (*mangling*) from the signature itself, so a C++ library can be called without a wrapper. A function inside a namespace is written with its path. extern \"C++\" \"./libmine.so\" { fn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 fn math::mul(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 } Python `py::` runs Python in a separate process and talks to it, so any library that can be `import`ed can be used: numpy, pandas, requests, PIL, and so on. fn times10(x) { return x * 10 } wok(py::eval(\"sum(range(10))\")) wok(py::call(\"statistics\", \"median\", [3, 1, 4, 1, 5])) wok(py::call(\"builtins\", \"list\", py::call(\"builtins\", \"map\", times10, [1, 2, 3])?)) let set = py::eval(\"{1, 2}\")? wok(typeof(set), py::method(set, \"issubset\", [1, 2, 3])) Result::Ok(45) Result::Ok(3) Result::Ok([10, 20, 30]) py:set Result::Ok(true) Values that have a counterpart (numb"}, {"j": "C, Rust, and Go", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html#c-rust-and-go", "g": "Other languages", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "C++", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html#c", "g": "Other languages", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Python", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html#python", "g": "Other languages", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "JavaScript, PHP, and Lua", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html#javascript-php-and-lua", "g": "Other languages", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Java and Kotlin", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html#java-and-kotlin", "g": "Other languages", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": ".NET", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html#net", "g": "Other languages", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "WebAssembly", "u": "web_dokumentasi/ffi.html#webassembly", "g": "Other languages", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "jrgc", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Running, checking, testing, and compiling .jrg files.", "t": "Running jrgc program.jrg # interpreter jrgc program.jrg --vm # VM bytecode jrgc program.jrg --jit # VM plus machine code for the hot parts jrgc program.jrg --emit-llvm # native, through clang jrgc program.jrg -- a b c # arguments for the program, read through env::args() jrgc program.jrg --watch # rerun every time the file changes jrgc # REPL Checking without running jrgc program.jrg --periksa # parse and check the file and all its imports; silent when clean jrgc program.jrg --fmt # print the tidied version to stdout (the file is not changed) jrgc program.jrg --emit-ast # print the syntax tree jrgc program.jrg --emit-tokens `--periksa` (check) finds syntax errors, undeclared names, and assignments to things that are not variables, before a single line runs. Tests and profiling jrgc calc.jrg --uji # run every fn test_*/uji_* jrgc calc.jrg --uji --uji-filter add jrgc program.jrg --profile # time per function after the program ends jrgc program.jrg --profile-json p.json # the JSON version Finding functions jrgc --cari word # built-in names containing \"word\" jrgc --doc str::split # signature and a short explanation jrgc --daftar-builtin # every name, one per line Native compilation `--emit-llvm` translates the program to LLVM IR and hands it to `clang`. The native runtime is built once with cargo and cached. jrgc program.jrg --emit-llvm -o program --no-run # compile only ./program jrgc program.jrg --emit-llvm -O3 --native-cpu --lto thin jrgc program.jrg --emit-llvm --full-power #"}, {"j": "Running", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#running", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Checking without running", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#checking-without-running", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Tests and profiling", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#tests-and-profiling", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Finding functions", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#finding-functions", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Native compilation", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#native-compilation", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "WebAssembly (in progress)", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#webassembly-in-progress", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Error messages", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#error-messages", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Other", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgc.html#other", "g": "jrgc", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "jrgm", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgm.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Managing projects: creating, running, testing, formatting, checking, dependencies, and releases.", "t": "A new project $ jrgm new calc Created `calc` (bin) package $ cd calc $ jrgm run Halo dari JawaRustGo++! What it contains: calc/ ├── jrgm.toml project manifest ├── src/main.jrg entry point └── tests/dasar.jrg tests [package] name = \"calc\" version = \"0.1.0\" edition = \"jrg2025\" entry = \"src/main.jrg\" [dependencies] helper = { path = \"../helper\" } `jrgm new name --kind lib` makes a library, `--kind web` a web application, and `--android` adds an `[android]` section. Projects in other languages work too: `jrgm new tool --bahasa python` (also `javascript`, `c`, `cpp`, `rust`, `go`, `java`, `php`, `lua`, `ruby`, `sh`); `run`, `build`, `test`, and `fmt` then use that language's tools. Commands Command What it is for --- --- `jrgm run [--vm | --jit] [--watch]` run the project; `--jit` through the VM that compiles the hot parts, `--watch` reruns on every change `jrgm test [--filter word] [--vm | --jit]` run the tests in `tests/*.jrg` `jrgm build` check every file of the project and its dependencies `jrgm check` check the entry point only `jrgm fmt [--check]` tidy spacing and indentation in every file `jrgm lint [--ketat]` errors plus warnings `jrgm exec file` run one file without a project (`.jrg`, `.c`, `.py`, `.rs`, `.go`, …) `jrgm clean` delete build output `jrgm lsp` a language server for editors `jrgm apk`, `jrgm kunci`, `jrgm siapkan android` Android apps, see [Android apps](android.html) Tests A function named `test_…` or `uji_…` in `tests/*.jrg` is a test. Each file runs in its"}, {"j": "A new project", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgm.html#a-new-project", "g": "jrgm", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Commands", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgm.html#commands", "g": "jrgm", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Tests", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgm.html#tests", "g": "jrgm", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Lint", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgm.html#lint", "g": "jrgm", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Dependencies", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgm.html#dependencies", "g": "jrgm", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Publishing", "u": "web_dokumentasi/jrgm.html#publishing", "g": "jrgm", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Editor, REPL, and IDE", "u": "web_dokumentasi/editor.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "A terminal editor, a language server for your favourite editor, and JRG IDE, which runs in the browser and on phones.", "t": "jrgedit `jrgc --sunting` opens a nano-style full-screen editor. It is written in JRG and ships inside `jrgc`, so it is always there. jrgc --sunting program.jrg jrgc --sunting a.jrg b.jrg:120 # several files; open b.jrg at line 120 jrgc --sunting project/ # browse a folder Key What it does --- --- Ctrl + S / Ctrl + Q save / quit Ctrl + Z / Ctrl + Y undo / redo Ctrl + F / Ctrl + P find / replace Ctrl + R save and run; afterwards the cursor jumps to the error Alt + P check without running Ctrl + O open another file; Alt +arrow switches files Shift +arrow, Ctrl + C / X / V select, copy, cut, paste F1 or Esc the full list of keys It has syntax highlighting, self-closing brackets, indentation that follows the previous line, and mouse support. REPL `jrgc` with no arguments opens the REPL. Up/down arrows walk the history, an unclosed block continues on the next line, and an error does not end the session. Command What it does --- --- `:cari word`, `:doc name`, `:daftar ns` find built-in functions `:tipe expression` the type, without printing the value `:simpan file.jrg` write the session out as a runnable program `:riwayat`, `:hapus`, `:keluar` history, restart, quit Language server (LSP) `jrgc --lsp` (or `jrgm lsp`) is a Language Server Protocol server. An editor that supports LSP gets: errors and warnings as you type, name completion (every built-in included), hover information, go to definition, and a file's symbol list. For example, in Neovim: vim.lsp.start({ name = \"jrg\", cmd = "}, {"j": "jrgedit", "u": "web_dokumentasi/editor.html#jrgedit", "g": "Editor and IDE", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "REPL", "u": "web_dokumentasi/editor.html#repl", "g": "Editor and IDE", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Language server (LSP)", "u": "web_dokumentasi/editor.html#language-server-lsp", "g": "Editor and IDE", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "JRG IDE", "u": "web_dokumentasi/editor.html#jrg-ide", "g": "Editor and IDE", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Android apps", "u": "web_dokumentasi/android.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Packaging a JRG program or project as an APK, from canvas games to apps with a web interface.", "t": "From one file jrgc game.jrg --apk game.apk # for phones (arm64-v8a) jrgc game.jrg --apk game.apk --apk-abi arm64-v8a,x86_64 adb install -r game.apk From a project jrgm siapkan android # once: download the platform, build-tools, and NDK jrgm new mygame --android cd mygame jrgm apk # target/mygame.apk jrgm run --android # install to the phone through adb, open it, follow its output The app's identity is written in `jrgm.toml`: [android] nama = \"My Game\" paket = \"id.jrg.mygame\" versi = \"1.2.0\" orientasi = \"portrait\" # bebas | portrait | landscape abi = [\"arm64-v8a\"] # add \"x86_64\" for the emulator ikon_depan = \"aset/ikon.png\" # Android 8+ adaptive icon ikon_latar = \"#0B1230\" # a colour or a PNG izin = [\"internet\", \"getar\"] Two kinds of display `tampilan` What it is Suits --- --- --- `\"kanvas\"` (default) the program is compiled natively through LLVM and draws full screen games, drawing, simple tools `\"web\"` `jrgc` ships inside the APK and runs your project as a local server; its pages show in a WebView apps with an HTML interface A project from `jrgm new x --kind web` already uses the web display. There the whole language and library are available, because it is run by the same interpreter as on a computer. The page also gets a `JRGAndroid` object to ask for permissions (`mintaIzin(\"kamera\")`), vibrate the phone, and exchange files with the phone's storage. The canvas display uses the LLVM backend, so that backend's limits apply (see [Not done yet](batasan.html)). Permissions `iz"}, {"j": "From one file", "u": "web_dokumentasi/android.html#from-one-file", "g": "Android apps", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "From a project", "u": "web_dokumentasi/android.html#from-a-project", "g": "Android apps", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Two kinds of display", "u": "web_dokumentasi/android.html#two-kinds-of-display", "g": "Android apps", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Permissions", "u": "web_dokumentasi/android.html#permissions", "g": "Android apps", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Languages and native libraries in the APK", "u": "web_dokumentasi/android.html#languages-and-native-libraries-in-the-apk", "g": "Android apps", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Releasing to the Play Store", "u": "web_dokumentasi/android.html#releasing-to-the-play-store", "g": "Android apps", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Four engines", "u": "web_dokumentasi/mesin.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "The interpreter, the bytecode VM, the JIT, and the LLVM backend, how all four are kept in agreement, and the fifth engine on the way.", "t": "All four engines share the same front end (lexer and parser), so their syntax and syntax-error messages are identical. They differ in how a program is run. Interpreter VM JIT LLVM native --- --- --- --- --- command `jrgc f.jrg` `jrgc f.jrg --vm` `jrgc f.jrg --jit` `jrgc f.jrg --emit-llvm` how it works walks the syntax tree directly translates to bytecode, then runs it a VM that compiles the hot parts to machine code while running translates to LLVM IR, which `clang` compiles to machine code language all of it all of it all of it every conformance file, not the whole library yet goroutines OS threads OS threads OS threads coroutines on one thread good for trying things, scripts programs that need more speed heavy loops with no compile step finished programs, standalone binaries, canvas APKs One program, one meaning A program that runs on two engines must print exactly the same output, including its error messages, their column, and the moment the error happens. When two engines answer differently, one of them is broken and gets fixed; it is never left as \"an implementation difference\". This is guarded by tables of programs that are run on several engines at once, with their output compared: Table Contents Count --- --- --- interpreter = VM programs whose output must be identical 617 rejected by both programs both engines must reject with the right message 365 interpreter = LLVM programs whose output must be identical 311 not yet on LLVM programs LLVM refuses with a clear reaso"}, {"j": "One program, one meaning", "u": "web_dokumentasi/mesin.html#one-program-one-meaning", "g": "Four engines", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Interpreter", "u": "web_dokumentasi/mesin.html#interpreter", "g": "Four engines", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "VM", "u": "web_dokumentasi/mesin.html#vm", "g": "Four engines", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "JIT", "u": "web_dokumentasi/mesin.html#jit", "g": "Four engines", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "LLVM native", "u": "web_dokumentasi/mesin.html#llvm-native", "g": "Four engines", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "WebAssembly (the fifth engine, in progress)", "u": "web_dokumentasi/mesin.html#webassembly-the-fifth-engine-in-progress", "g": "Four engines", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Memory model", "u": "web_dokumentasi/memori.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "Values never share storage, and a copy is only made when someone writes.", "t": "Values never share Assigning, passing to a function, returning, or reading from a collection always gives a value that stands on its own. Changing a copy never changes the original. var a = [1, 2, 3] var b = a b[0] = 99 fn change(xs) { var ys = xs ys[1] = 0 return ys } let c = change(a) wok(a, b, c) Because every value has exactly one owner, it lives exactly as long as the variable that owns it. JRG needs no garbage collector, and ordinary values have no dangling pointers. Copying is cheap The real copy is only made when someone writes. `var b = a` copies nothing; `b[0] = 99` is what copies. As a result: passing a large array to a function that only reads it copies nothing; `a = a.push(x)`, `a = a + [x]`, and `d = dict::set(d, k, v)` change in place, so adding elements one at a time stays linear; `a[i] = v` does not depend on the size of `a`. What really is expensive: adding at the front (`a = [x] + a`), because it moves every element. For a double-ended queue, use `std/deque`. What shares on purpose A few things are designed to share, and all of them are plain to see in the code: What Example --- --- variables captured by a closure `let naik = || { n += 1 }` changes the outer `n` synchronisation handles `Atomic`, `Mutex`, channels explicit handles `ptr::new(value)` manual allocation `jawa::alloc` and `jawir::free` objects from other languages `py::`, `js::`, `jvm::` handles let p = ptr::new(0) let q = p ptr::set(q, 42) wok(ptr::get(p)) let m = jawa::alloc(7) jawir::free(m) w"}, {"j": "Values never share", "u": "web_dokumentasi/memori.html#values-never-share", "g": "Memory model", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Copying is cheap", "u": "web_dokumentasi/memori.html#copying-is-cheap", "g": "Memory model", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "What shares on purpose", "u": "web_dokumentasi/memori.html#what-shares-on-purpose", "g": "Memory model", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "One known leak", "u": "web_dokumentasi/memori.html#one-known-leak", "g": "Memory model", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Diagnostics", "u": "web_dokumentasi/diagnostik.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "The shape of JRG's error messages, their tone, and how to use them from an editor or CI.", "t": "The shape of a message Below, `totl` was typed where `total` was meant: kepentok di baris 3 waktu jalan. keterangannya di bawah. error: variabel `totl` belum dikenal — maksudnya `total`? kalau bukan, cek lagi penulisannya atau jangan-jangan lupa `let totl = ...` dulu --> calc.jrg:3:9 | 3 | wok(totl) | ^^^^ | **A greeting**, one line at the top. The wording varies; `--sopan` drops it. **What is wrong**, with the name you wrote and, when there is one, a spelling suggestion. **Where**: file, line, and column. **A code excerpt** with `^` under the part at fault. **`bantuan:`** (help) gives the way out, and **`catatan:`** (note) extra context, when there is any. **`jejak:`** (trace) for run-time errors: where the failing function was called from. Messages are in Indonesian today. English messages are on the way, chosen once with `jrgm language en`. A message names a value's **type**, not its contents. Misusing an array of three numbers or of three thousand gives an equally short message. Tone The first time an error appears it is always delivered seriously. When the same error comes back, the greeting changes according to the chosen tone, and at the seventh repetition a help line is added, because the same message seven times means the explanation has not landed yet. jrgc f.jrg --nada hangat # sopan | galak (default) | judes | hangat | cool | lawak JRG_NADA=cool jrgc f.jrg jrgc f.jrg --sopan # no greeting at all Tone only changes the greeting line. The body of the message is alway"}, {"j": "The shape of a message", "u": "web_dokumentasi/diagnostik.html#the-shape-of-a-message", "g": "Diagnostics", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Tone", "u": "web_dokumentasi/diagnostik.html#tone", "g": "Diagnostics", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "For editors and CI", "u": "web_dokumentasi/diagnostik.html#for-editors-and-ci", "g": "Diagnostics", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "No silent wrong results", "u": "web_dokumentasi/diagnostik.html#no-silent-wrong-results", "g": "Diagnostics", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Benchmark", "u": "web_dokumentasi/benchmark.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "How fast JRG's native backend is compared with C and Go, and how to measure it yourself.", "t": "The figures below are from the LLVM backend (`--emit-llvm`), measured on an Intel i5-1235U with clang 21, rustc 1.97, and Go 1.26. Each figure is the best time of six rounds, pinned to one CPU core. Kernel JRG (ms) C (ms) JRG : C --- --- --- --- `numeric_kernel` 732 748 0.98 `call_recursion` 595 589 1.01 `struct_particles` 1044 981 1.06 `array_sort` 1060 880 1.20 `match_dispatch` 788 585 1.35 `mixed_report` 523 243 2.15 `string_ops` 516 147 3.51 In short: on a par with C for numbers and function calls, a little behind for structs, arrays, and `match`, and clearly slower for text processing. Against Go, JRG wins three kernels (`struct_particles` 5.8 times, `call_recursion` 1.5 times, `match_dispatch` 1.45 times), ties two, and loses two. The gap in `string_ops` comes from temporary strings that are not freed while the program runs; that is the next piece of work for this backend. Measuring it yourself ROUNDS=5 CPU=0 benchmarks/llvm/run.sh For the interpreter and the VM there are nine separate kernels: sh benchmarks/acuan/jalankan.sh \"$PWD/target/release/jrgc\" 3 Differences below about 6–10 percent cannot be told apart from measurement noise on an ordinary machine. To prove a change is faster, count its instructions with `valgrind --tool=callgrind`, rather than only comparing clocks."}, {"j": "Measuring it yourself", "u": "web_dokumentasi/benchmark.html#measuring-it-yourself", "g": "Benchmark", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Not done yet", "u": "web_dokumentasi/batasan.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "The known shortcomings of JRG 0.6.0.", "t": "Language **Enum discriminants** (`enum E { A = 5 }`) do not exist yet. Map variants to numbers with a `match`. **No `async`/`await`.** Concurrency uses goroutines and channels; each goroutine is an OS thread, so it suits tens to hundreds of tasks, not millions. **Type annotations are not enforced.** The type name is checked, the type itself is not. **`for` with one name gives the index**, ranges included. This is deliberate, but often surprising; see [Control flow](kendali.html#for). **A closure that calls itself** is never freed from memory. Use a named `fn`. **Error messages are in Indonesian.** English messages are on the way (`jrgm language en`). Backend LLVM The interpreter and the VM run the whole language. The LLVM backend runs every conformance file, and refuses (with its reason) a few shapes: spread (`f(...v)`) and named arguments (`f(a: 1)`) to a closure held in a variable or parameter (to a named function they work); calling a function stored as a tagged value (for example inside a mixed array); some `std/` modules written very dynamically; integers above 64 bits. Canvas programs packaged as an APK use this backend, so the same limits apply there. Library **Time is always UTC.** `time::format` does not know time zones yet. **Canvas windows** need a build with `--features canvas`; by default it draws to a file or to the browser. **No central package registry yet.** Dependencies come from local folders or git repositories. **Java/Kotlin FFI is not available inside an"}, {"j": "Language", "u": "web_dokumentasi/batasan.html#language", "g": "Not done yet", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Backend LLVM", "u": "web_dokumentasi/batasan.html#backend-llvm", "g": "Not done yet", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Library", "u": "web_dokumentasi/batasan.html#library", "g": "Not done yet", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Tools and platforms", "u": "web_dokumentasi/batasan.html#tools-and-platforms", "g": "Not done yet", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Release notes 0.6.0", "u": "web_dokumentasi/rilis.html", "g": "Docs", "k": "The sixth release of JawaRustGo++.", "t": "0.6.0 is JRG's sixth release. It makes `--jit` much faster for code that lives on structs and `map`/`filter`/`reduce`, brings `--jit` to `jrgm`, and tightens some resource limits. Three changes can make old programs behave differently; they are listed below. Faster `--jit` now computes small formula methods right where they are called. `x = x.m(dt)` that builds a new `Self` from a float formula over its fields no longer calls the method and builds a new struct: the fields are read, computed in registers, and written back into the same struct. A method that only returns a float formula over its fields (`rec.score()`) is computed in place too. Callbacks of `map`/`filter`/`reduce` and friends are called without setting up a new frame for every element. Wall-clock time against 0.5.0: program 0.5.0 0.6.0 --- --- --- `struct_particles` 12.9 s 3.3 s `mixed_report` 2.2 s 1.4 s `hof_closure` 0.66 s 0.44 s `string_scan` 0.44 s 0.32 s The answers stay exactly the same as the interpreter's and the VM's, including when the struct is shared with another variable, a field changes type, or a callback fails halfway through an array. New `jrgm run --jit`, `jrgm exec --jit`, and `jrgm test --jit`: a project can be run and tested through the fourth engine, the same as `jrgc --jit`. `--watch` passes it on too. May break old programs `json::stringify`, `json::pretty`, and `dok::json_encode` refuse library handles (`Ptr`, `Future`, `DokDb`, …), like `Mutex`. They used to produce `{\"id\":1}`. `dok::s"}, {"j": "Faster", "u": "web_dokumentasi/rilis.html#faster", "g": "Release notes", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "New", "u": "web_dokumentasi/rilis.html#new", "g": "Release notes", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "May break old programs", "u": "web_dokumentasi/rilis.html#may-break-old-programs", "g": "Release notes", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Fixed", "u": "web_dokumentasi/rilis.html#fixed", "g": "Release notes", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Installing", "u": "web_dokumentasi/rilis.html#installing", "g": "Release notes", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "Not yet", "u": "web_dokumentasi/rilis.html#not-yet", "g": "Release notes", "k": "", "t": ""}, {"j": "About the author", "u": "profil.html", "g": "Profile", "k": "Muhamad Surya Widianto, creator of JawaRustGo++", "t": " \n   \n   \n     Creator of JawaRustGo++ \n     Muhamad Surya Widianto \n     Student of Telecommunication and Informatics Business Management Faculty of Economics and Business, Telkom University \n     Bogor, West Java, Indonesia \n     \n       LinkedIn \n       Send an email \n       GitHub \n     \n   \n \n\n \n   About \n   I study Telecommunication and Informatics Business Management at Telkom University, and I am from Bogor. I like\n  taking apart how things work, from software to how modern businesses run, and then trying to build them myself. \n   Outside class, most of my time goes into writing code. JawaRustGo++ is the biggest result so far. \n \n\n \n   Projects \n   \n     JawaRustGo++ (JRG) \n     A programming language written in Rust, with error messages that explain the way out. One program runs t"}, {"j": "Start Here", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/index.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "One page to understand what this site holds and choose where to begin. Read this first, five minutes, before jumping to any chapter.", "t": "What this site is Two things are taught here at once: school mathematics and JRG , a programming language. They are taught together because each helps the other &mdash; writing a formula as code forces us to understand it down to the details, and the computer tells us right away when our understanding is wrong. You do not need to install anything. Every code example has a Run button that really runs the JRG code, right here in this browser, and an Edit button to change it first. Two paths, pick the one that fits How to read the coloured boxes Across this site, colours have fixed meanings. Once you know these four, you can tell how important a box is before reading it. Teal: something worth remembering. An explanation, a trick, or background that makes something else make sense. Orange: the most common mistake in that section. If your answer is off, check this box first. Red: something that runs without an error message but gives a wrong result. This is the most dangerous kind, because nothing warns you. Yellow: a real-world use, or a pointer to the next chapter. The tools on these pages Try running wok(7 / 2) . A calculator says 3.5 &mdash; JRG answers 3 , because dividing two whole numbers gives a whole number. Differences like that are shown, not hidden. If you get stuck The third step sounds trivial and is actually the most powerful. Chapter JRG 12 goes deeper into it. If you are completely new, open JRG Basics &mdash; Chapter 1 in the menu on the left. If you want to go s"}, {"j": "First Program", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg1.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Writing, running, and reading the result. No math in this chapter &mdash; just making sure you can get the computer to follow one sentence.", "t": "One line that produces something A program is a list of commands carried out in order, from top to bottom. The most basic command in JRG is wok &mdash; it means “write this to the screen”. pub fn main() { wok(\"Hello, world!\") } Three things there, and only one of them really does the work: A short word that is easy to say, and deliberately short because it is the command you will write most while learning. Its counterparts are print in Python, console.log in JavaScript, and println! in Rust. Writing several things at once wok accepts more than one value. The values are separated by spaces automatically. pub fn main() { wok(\"Score:\", 90, \"out of\", 100) wok(\"Difference:\", 100 - 90) } To continue on the same line, use wok_ (with the underscore). Handy for printing numbers side by side. pub fn main() { wok_(\"1\", \" \") wok_(\"2\", \" \") wok_(\"3\", \" \") wok(\"\") } Comments: notes for humans A line starting with // is not run by the computer. Use it to explain why , not to repeat what . pub fn main() { // The price already includes tax, so it is not multiplied again. wok(\"Total:\", 120000) /* A long comment may span several lines. */ } When something is wrong JRG does not stay quiet when your program is wrong. It names the line, points at the column, and gives a suggestion. Try a deliberate mistake (error messages are in Indonesian for now; the line, the column, and the ^ say where): pub fn main() { wok(\"forgot the closing bracket\" } Read it from the bottom up: which line, then the ^ mark "}, {"j": "Variables & Types", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg2.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "How the computer remembers. And one thing that trips many people up at the start: JRG tells whole numbers apart from numbers with a decimal point.", "t": "Storing a value under a name A variable is like a labelled box. You put a value in it, then call it up by its name. pub fn main() { let name = \"Sari\" let age = 17 wok(name, \"is\", age, \"years old\") } Three keywords, and the difference is real This is not a formality. If you write let and then try to change it, JRG refuses &mdash; and that saves you from changing something you did not mean to. pub fn main() { var score = 0 score = score + 10 score += 5 // means the same as the line above wok(\"Score:\", score) } pub fn main() { let score = 0 score = 10 } Writing let and then wanting to change the value. Change it to var &mdash; the error message says so directly (its bantuan: line, “help”). int and float are two different things The numbers 7 and 7.0 look like the same value, but JRG treats them differently. The first is a whole number ( int ), the second a number with a decimal point ( float ). pub fn main() { wok(typeof(7), typeof(7.0)) wok(7.0) // a float always shows its decimal point wok(1 + 2.0) // mixed -> the result is a float } Dividing two whole numbers gives a whole number &mdash; the remainder is thrown away , not rounded. So 7 / 2 is 3 , not 3.5 and not 4. If you want 3.5, one of the sides has to be a float. pub fn main() { wok(7 / 2) // int / int -> int wok(7.0 / 2.0) // float / float -> float wok(7 as float / 2) // turned into a float first } Changing type with as pub fn main() { let x = 4.9 wok(x as int) // cut off, not rounded wok(4 as float) wok(math::round(x)) "}, {"j": "Operators & Arithmetic", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg3.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Every way to calculate in JRG, including the remainder, which turns out to be far more useful than it looks.", "t": "Arithmetic operators pub fn main() { wok(7 + 2, 7 - 2, 7 * 2) wok(7 / 2, 7 % 2, 2 ** 10) } The remainder ( % ) is a multi-purpose tool The remainder answers: “if this is shared out evenly, how much is left over?” Many tricks are born from this one operator. pub fn main() { let n = 18 wok(\"Even?\", n % 2 == 0) wok(\"Divisible by 3?\", n % 3 == 0) wok(\"Last digit:\", n % 10) } n % 2 == 0 &rarr; even. n % k == 0 &rarr; divisible by k. n % 10 &rarr; the last digit. All three keep coming up in programming problems. Order of operations Just as in math: powers first, then multiply/divide, then add/subtract. Brackets beat everything. pub fn main() { wok(2 + 3 * 4) // multiply first wok((2 + 3) * 4) // brackets first wok(2 ** 3 ** 2) // powers from the right: 2 ** 9 } 2 ** 3 ** 2 means 2 ** (3 ** 2) = 2 ** 9 = 512, not (2**3)**2 = 64. That matches the math rule, and differs from the other operators, which bind from the left. Comparing pub fn main() { wok(7 > 2, 7 == 7.0, \"a\" == \"a\") wok([1, 2] == [1, 2]) // arrays are compared by their contents } Combining conditions &amp;&amp; means “and” (both must be true). || means “or” (one is enough). ! flips it. pub fn main() { let age = 17 let hasId = false wok(\"May enter?\", age >= 17 && hasId) wok(\"Needs checking?\", age < 17 || !hasId) } Compound operators Writing score = score + 10 feels long. There is a short form: pub fn main() { var n = 10 n += 5 n -= 3 n *= 2 n /= 4 wok(n) } What is 17 % 5 ? What is 2 + 3 * 4 ? How do you check that a number"}, {"j": "Branching: if & match", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg4.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Making a program choose a path. Two ways: one for free-form conditions, one for matching values.", "t": " if &mdash; if this, do that pub fn main() { let score = 82 if score >= 75 { wok(\"Passed\") } else { wok(\"Not passed yet\") } } Unlike C or Java, an if condition in JRG does not need to be wrapped in brackets. What is required is the curly braces { } , even for a single line &mdash; that closes off a whole class of classic bugs. Several branches fn grade(n: int) -> string { if n >= 90 { return \"A\" } else if n >= 80 { return \"B\" } else if n >= 70 { return \"C\" } else { return \"D\" } } pub fn main() { wok(95, \"->\", grade(95)) wok(83, \"->\", grade(83)) wok(60, \"->\", grade(60)) } Branches are checked from the top. If n >= 70 were placed first, a score of 95 would stop there and get a “C”. Arrange them from the strictest condition down. if also produces a value In JRG, if is not just a statement &mdash; it can be used directly as a value. pub fn main() { let age = 20 let status = if age >= 17 { \"adult\" } else { \"child\" } wok(status) } For a simple choice there is the compact form condition $ a : b . Read it as: “if the condition holds, take a, otherwise b”. It opens with $ , not ? &mdash; in JRG the question mark is used for something else. pub fn main() { let n = 7 wok(n % 2 == 0 $ \"even\" : \"odd\") } match &mdash; matching a value When you compare one value against many possibilities, match is far tidier than a pile of else if . fn dayName(n: int) -> string { return match n { 1 => \"Monday\", 2 => \"Tuesday\", 3 => \"Wednesday\", 6 | 7 => \"Weekend\", _ => \"Another day\" } } pub fn main() { wok"}, {"j": "Loops", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg5.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Repeating work without copying code. This chapter holds one JRG-specific thing that, if you do not know it, lets your program run smoothly while answering wrong.", "t": "The first thing you must know about for In many languages, for x in list gives you the contents of the list. In JRG, it gives you the position number (the index). pub fn main() { let scores = [70, 85, 90] for x in scores { wok_(x, \" \") } wok(\"\") } The program above does not error . It runs, prints numbers, and exits calmly &mdash; the numbers are just wrong. If you used it to add up scores, you would get the total of 0+1+2 , not of the real scores. The form that binds the contents uses two names, separated by a comma: pub fn main() { let scores = [70, 85, 90] for i, x in scores { wok(\"Item\", i, \"holds\", x) } } If you do not need the position number, write _ in its place. That mark means “it is there, but deliberately unused”. pub fn main() { let scores = [70, 85, 90] var total = 0 for _, x in scores { total += x } wok(\"Total:\", total, \" Average:\", total as float / len(scores) as float) } Number ranges work the same way 1..=5 makes a row of five numbers. But for n in 1..=5 still gives the index &mdash; that is, 0 to 4. pub fn main() { wok_(\"for n in 1..=5 -> \") for n in 1..=5 { wok_(n, \" \") } wok(\"\") wok_(\"for _, n in 1..=5 -> \") for _, n in 1..=5 { wok_(n, \" \") } wok(\"\") } If there is only one name after for , it always holds the position number &mdash; for arrays, strings, and ranges alike. Need the contents? Use two names. while &mdash; as long as the condition holds Use while when you do not know how many times to repeat, only when to stop. pub fn main() { var left = 100 v"}, {"j": "Functions", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg6.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Giving a name to a piece of work, so it can be called again and again without copying a single line.", "t": "The basic form fn squareArea(side: int) -> int { return side * side } pub fn main() { wok(squareArea(5)) wok(squareArea(12)) } Three reasons: no copying code, the function's name explains the intent, and when the formula changes you only fix one place. Several parameters fn triangleArea(base: float, height: float) -> float { return 0.5 * base * height } fn perimeter(w: int, h: int) -> int { return 2 * (w + h) } pub fn main() { wok(\"Triangle area:\", triangleArea(10.0, 6.0)) wok(\"Perimeter:\", perimeter(7, 4)) } Default parameter values A parameter may have a fallback value. If the caller does not fill it in, that value is used. fn greet(name: string, greeting: string = \"Hello\") -> string { return greeting + \", \" + name + \"!\" } pub fn main() { wok(greet(\"Sari\")) wok(greet(\"Budi\", \"Good morning\")) } Functions with no return value Not every function produces a value. Some only do something &mdash; printing, for example. fn printLine() { wok(\"------------------------\") } fn printTitle(text: string) { printLine() wok(text) printLine() } pub fn main() { printTitle(\"Score Report\") } Functions calling functions A function may call other functions, including itself. That last one is called recursion . fn factorial(n: int) -> int { if n <= 1 { return 1 } return n * factorial(n - 1) } pub fn main() { for _, i in 1..=6 { wok(i, \"! =\", factorial(i)) } } The line if n &lt;= 1 { return 1 } is the brake. Without a stopping condition, the function keeps calling itself until JRG stops it and rep"}, {"j": "Arrays & Text", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg7.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Keeping many values under one name, and working with sentences. The two types you will use most after numbers.", "t": "Arrays: a row of values pub fn main() { let scores = [70, 85, 90, 65] wok(scores) wok(\"How many:\", len(scores)) wok(\"The first:\", scores[0]) wok(\"The last:\", scores[len(scores) - 1]) } The first element is at position 0 , not 1 . So an array of 4 elements has positions 0, 1, 2, 3 &mdash; and scores[4] is an error. JRG has a shortcut for positions counted from the end: negative numbers. pub fn main() { let a = [10, 20, 30, 40] wok(a[-1], a[-2]) // from the end } pub fn main() { let a = [10, 20] wok(a[5]) } JRG never quietly returns an empty value for a position that does not exist. It stops and states the real length (\"isinya 2 elemen\" &mdash; it holds 2 elements) &mdash; far better than a program that carries on with a fake value. Slicing an array pub fn main() { let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] wok(a[0:3]) // the first three wok(a[3:]) // from position 3 to the end wok(a[:2]) // up to just before position 2 } Built-in functions for arrays pub fn main() { let n = [8, 3, 10, 1] wok(\"Sum :\", arr::sum(n)) wok(\"Average :\", arr::mean(n)) wok(\"Largest :\", arr::max(n)) wok(\"Sorted :\", arr::sort(n)) wok(\"Original:\", n) } Look at the last line: after arr::sort , n is still as it was. In JRG every value is owned by one name; functions give a new array. To keep the result, assign it back: n = arr::sort(n) . pub fn main() { var n = [8, 3, 10, 1] n = arr::sort(n) n = arr::push(n, 99) wok(n) } Text (strings) pub fn main() { let s = \"Learning JRG\" wok(\"Length :\", len(s)) wok(\"Upper case :\", str::"}, {"j": "Structs & Methods", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg8.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Making your own data type. So that a \"point\" is no longer two variables that happen to sit next to each other, but one whole thing.", "t": "The problem first Imagine you store a student's data: name, score, and attendance. Without a struct, the three become separate variables &mdash; and nothing ties them together. pub fn main() { let name = \"Sari\" let score = 88 let attendance = 95 wok(name, score, attendance) } Now imagine there are 30 students. A struct solves that: struct Student { pub name: string, pub score: int, pub attendance: int } pub fn main() { let s = Student { name: \"Sari\", score: 88, attendance: 95 } wok(s) wok(\"Name :\", s.name) wok(\"Score:\", s.score) } pub : fields that may be seen from outside Notice the word pub in front of each field. Without it, a field can only be read from inside that type's own methods &mdash; and JRG refuses if you try to read it from outside. struct Wallet { balance: int // no pub -> closed } pub fn main() { let w = Wallet { balance: 50000 } wok(w.balance) } A closed field forces data to change only through methods you write yourself &mdash; so the rule (say, \"the balance can never go negative\") lives in one place, not scattered over every line that touches it. struct Wallet { balance: int } impl Wallet { fn new(start: int) -> Wallet { return Wallet { balance: start } } fn look(self) -> int { return self.balance } fn withdraw(self, n: int) -> Wallet { if n > self.balance { return self } // the rule lives here only return Wallet { balance: self.balance - n } } } pub fn main() { var w = Wallet::new(50000) w = w.withdraw(20000) w = w.withdraw(999999) // quietly refused by th"}, {"j": "Functions as Values", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg9.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "In JRG a function is not just something you call &mdash; it can be stored in a variable, handed to another function, and made on the spot.", "t": "Storing a function in a variable fn twice(n: int) -> int { return n * 2 } pub fn main() { let f = twice // stored, not called yet wok(f(21)) wok(typeof(f)) } Closures: functions without a name If a function is only used once, it does not need a name. The short form uses a pair of vertical bars. pub fn main() { let square = |x| x * x let add = |a, b| a + b wok(square(7)) wok(add(3, 4)) } Because it \"closes over\" the values around it &mdash; variables from outside stay readable inside it, even after that line has passed. pub fn main() { let tax = 0.11 let withTax = |price| price * (1.0 + tax) wok(withTax(100000.0)) } Three functions that change how you write pub fn main() { let n = [12, 7, 25, 3, 18] wok(\"Squared :\", arr::map(n, |x| x * x)) wok(\"The even:\", arr::filter(n, |x| x % 2 == 0)) wok(\"Summed :\", arr::reduce(n, |a, b| a + b, 0)) } Compare it with writing a loop &mdash; both are correct, but the one above states what you want, not how to do it. pub fn main() { let n = [12, 7, 25, 3, 18] var even = [] for _, x in n { if x % 2 == 0 { even = arr::push(even, x) } } wok(\"With a loop :\", even) wok(\"With a filter:\", arr::filter(n, |x| x % 2 == 0)) } Chaining several steps pub fn main() { let scores = [45, 88, 92, 67, 79, 95] let passed = arr::filter(scores, |n| n >= 75) let bonus = arr::map(passed, |n| n + 5) let total = arr::reduce(bonus, |a, b| a + b, 0) wok(\"Passed :\", passed) wok(\"Bonus :\", bonus) wok(\"Total :\", total) wok(\"Average:\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", total as float / l"}, {"j": "When the Answer Might Not Exist", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg10.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "How JRG handles \"not found\" and \"failed\" &mdash; without ever handing over a fake value that looks like an answer.", "t": "The problem being solved What should a search function return when the thing it looks for is not there? Many languages answer null &mdash; and that is where the trouble starts, because null looks like an ordinary value until the program has already used it. JRG answers differently: such a function returns an Option , which holds one of two things &mdash; Some(value) if it was found, or None if it was not. pub fn main() { let a = [10, 20, 30] wok(a.first()) wok(a.get(1)) wok(a.get(99)) // out of range -> None, not a guess } Taking the value out safely pub fn main() { let a = [10, 20, 30] wok(a.get(1).unwrap_or(0)) wok(a.get(99).unwrap_or(0)) match a.get(99) { Some(v) => wok(\"Found:\", v), None => wok(\"Nothing at that position\") } } a ?? b means \"use a if it has a value, otherwise use b \". The difference from unwrap_or : b is only computed when it is actually needed . pub fn main() { let a = [10, 20] wok(a.get(0) ?? -1) wok(a.get(9) ?? -1) } Result : success, or failure with a reason When a failure has a cause that needs explaining, JRG uses Result : Ok(value) or Err(reason) . fn divide(a: int, b: int) -> Result { if b == 0 { return Err(\"cannot divide by zero\") } return Ok(a / b) } pub fn main() { match divide(10, 2) { Ok(v) => wok(\"Result:\", v), Err(e) => wok(\"Failed:\", e) } match divide(10, 0) { Ok(v) => wok(\"Result:\", v), Err(e) => wok(\"Failed:\", e) } } Passing a failure on with ? Writing a match for every call is tiring. The ? mark means: \"if this failed, stop this function "}, {"j": "Dicts: Paired Data", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg11.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Storing data that is looked up by name, not by position. Like a dictionary: you look up a word, you get its meaning.", "t": "When an array is not enough Arrays are good for ordered lists. But if you want to store a value per name &mdash; Sari's score, Budi's score &mdash; a position number does not help. pub fn main() { let scores = {\"Sari\": 88, \"Budi\": 76, \"Tono\": 91} wok(scores) wok(\"Sari's score:\", scores[\"Sari\"]) wok(\"How many:\", len(scores)) } Adding, changing, checking pub fn main() { var scores = {\"Sari\": 88} scores[\"Budi\"] = 76 // add scores[\"Sari\"] = 90 // change wok(scores) wok(\"Is Tono there?\", dict::has(scores, \"Tono\")) wok(\"Tono's score:\", dict::get(scores, \"Tono\")) } Just like a.get(i) on an array: if the key is not there, the answer is None &mdash; not a zero pretending to be a real value. Walking through a dict As with arrays, the two-name form gives the key and its value. pub fn main() { let scores = {\"Sari\": 88, \"Budi\": 76, \"Tono\": 91} for name, n in scores { wok(name, \"->\", n) } } A dict is printed and walked in a fixed key order, so your program's output does not change from run to run. That matters when you compare the result or save it to a file. Keys are always text Whatever you use as a key is stored as its written form. So d[0] and d[\"0\"] point at the same place. pub fn main() { var d = dict::new() d[1] = \"one\" wok(d) wok(d[\"1\"]) // the same key } A real example: counting frequencies pub fn main() { let words = [\"apple\", \"orange\", \"apple\", \"mango\", \"apple\", \"orange\"] var count = dict::new() for _, w in words { if dict::has(count, w) { count[w] = count[w] + 1 } else { count["}, {"j": "Reading Error Messages", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/jrg12.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "The skill that makes you independent fastest. Every message in this chapter comes straight from JRG &mdash; none of them is rewritten. JRG's messages are in Indonesian for now (English messages are on the way), so each one is explained in English underneath.", "t": "An error is not a sign that you failed Error messages are the one part of a programming language that really teaches . They know exactly what is wrong and where. All you need to do is read them to the end &mdash; most people stop at the first line. Every JRG error has four parts: A mistyped name pub fn main() { let total = 10 wok(totl) } Look for the part that names a similar name ( maksudnya `total`? &mdash; “did you mean `total`?”). If a name is only one letter off, JRG suggests it &mdash; so you do not have to check every name one by one. A bracket that was never closed pub fn main() { wok(\"hello\" } Here the pointer is on the } of line 3 (“there should be a ) here, but a } was found”), while what is missing is the ) on line 2. That is natural: JRG only notices something is missing when it finds a mark that does not fit. If the pointer is on a closing bracket, check the line above it. Reaching outside the range pub fn main() { let a = [10, 20, 30] wok(a[7]) } Notice that the message states the real length (“this array holds 3 elements, valid indices 0..2”). That usually tells you the cause right away: a counter that is one too high, or data that is shorter than you expected. Changing a value that may not change pub fn main() { let limit = 100 limit = 200 } “ limit was declared with let , so it cannot be reassigned”, and the bantuan: line says to use var or let mut if the value really has to change. Types that do not fit together pub fn main() { wok(5 % \"two\") } JRG delibera"}, {"j": "Numbers", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch1.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Before calculating anything, we need to agree on what a number is, and how many \"kinds\" of number there are. This chapter is the foundation — understand it well here, and the following chapters get much lighter.", "t": "The big family of numbers Every number you have ever written belongs to one of the following \"families\". The further down the table, the bigger the family — each family contains the families above it. Because different families follow different rules. Dividing two integers does not always give an integer (7 ÷ 2 = 3.5), but dividing two natural numbers with a remainder of zero always does. Knowing the \"kind\" of number you hold helps you guess the kind of answer. The number line The easiest way to picture integers is as equally spaced points on a line. Zero in the middle, positives to the right, negatives to the left. The further right, the bigger the value — not the \"more digits\". This matters: −1 is bigger than −100 , even though \"100\" looks bigger than \"1\". Place value The number 2507 is really shorthand for a sum based on the position of each digit: Thinking the \"0\" is unimportant and can be dropped. But 2507 and 257 are different numbers — the zero holds the other digits in place. Number types in JRG In programming, these \"kinds of number\" have their own technical name: data types . JRG has two main number types — int for integers, and float for numbers with a decimal point. pub fn main() { // int → an integer, no decimal point let age: int = 17 // float → a number with a decimal point let height: float = 165.5 let name = \"Budi\" wok(\"Age:\", age) wok(\"Height:\", height, \"cm\") wok(\"Zero is still a number:\", 0) } The computer stores them differently in memory. An int is more c"}, {"j": "Addition & Subtraction", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch2.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "The two most basic operations — but with properties that, once mastered, make mental arithmetic much faster than relying on a calculator.", "t": "Three properties of addition When adding many numbers, you are free to group the easy ones first. Adding 28 + 47 + 2 ? Group 28 + 2 first (=30), then 30 + 47 = 77 . Much faster than going left to right. Subtraction = adding the opposite The most important idea in this chapter: a − b is the same as a + (−b) . That is why subtraction is not commutative and not associative — you are really adding numbers whose signs depend on the order. A quick-arithmetic trick: rounding to the tens For something like 47 + 38 , do not calculate it as it stands. Round one number to the nearest ten first, then correct: \"Andi has 10 apples and gives 4 to a friend\" → many students write 4 − 10 because of the order of the numbers in the sentence. The right way: find the starting amount (10) minus the amount lost (4) → 10 − 4 = 6 . The order in a word problem ≠ the order in the formula. Variables that change: mut and the short operators In JRG, an ordinary variable ( let ) cannot change its value once defined. For a variable whose value will change — like a balance or a score — you must mark it mut (mutable), or declare it with var . pub fn main() { let mut balance = 50000 wok(\"Starting balance:\", balance) balance += 20000 // same as balance = balance + 20000 wok(\"After saving:\", balance) balance -= 35000 // same as balance = balance - 35000 wok(\"After shopping:\", balance) } Writing balance += 20000 on a variable declared with plain let makes JRG refuse the program before it runs — the error says that"}, {"j": "Multiplication & Division", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch3.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Multiplication is \"repeated addition, sped up\". Division hides one idea that is used constantly in programming: the remainder.", "t": "Multiplication as repeated addition Like addition, multiplication is commutative (a×b=b×a), associative ((a×b)×c=a×(b×c)), and has an identity — but the identity is 1 , not 0, because a × 1 = a . For 9 × n (n = 1 to 10): the answer is always (n−1) in front, then (10−n) behind. For example 9×7 : front = 6, back = 3 → 63 . Try 9×4 : front=3, back=6 → 36 . ✓ Division: quotient vs remainder When 17 is divided by 5, the result is not a tidy whole number. There are two ways to look at it: The remainder (modulo) is \"what is left over\" after sharing out evenly as much as possible. It is an idea you will use again and again — to check even/odd, to take turns, to work with clocks (24 hours repeating), and much more. Dividing by zero. Mathematically, a ÷ 0 is undefined — not \"infinity\", not \"zero\", it really has no answer. A program that tries it usually crashes or raises an error, rather than quietly giving a strange result. The % operator for checking even and odd In JRG (as in most programming languages), the modulo symbol is % . It is one of the most used operators — far more often than it looks in a math class. fn isEven(n: int) -> bool { return n % 2 == 0 } pub fn main() { let number = 234 let quotient = number / 6 let remainder = number % 6 wok(number, \"/ 6 = \", quotient, \"remainder\", remainder) wok(\"Is it even?\", isEven(number)) } An analogue clock \"goes round\" every 12 — that is modulo 12. A game grid that \"wraps around\" to the other side — that is modulo the grid width. Sharin"}, {"j": "Order of Operations", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch4.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "When one problem has addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, and brackets all at once — the math world has agreed on the order to work them out. Without this rule, one problem could have several conflicting \"right answers\".", "t": "The PEMDAS / BODMAS rule These two acronyms teach the same order, with different words: Multiplication &amp; division have equal rank — they go from left to right, not \"multiplication always first\". The same goes for addition &amp; subtraction. So 20 ÷ 4 × 2 = (20÷4)×2 = 10, not 20÷(4×2)=2.5. An example, step by step Working left to right regardless of the kind of operation (8+2=10 first, then ×2... and so on). That gives a completely wrong answer. The order of kinds of operation always beats the order of where they are written . JRG follows exactly the same rule Programming-language compilers and interpreters, JRG included, evaluate expressions with operator precedence rules that copy ordinary math. Brackets are still the safest way to make sure the order is what you mean. pub fn main() { let a = 8 + 2 * 5 // no brackets let b = (8 + 2) * 5 // with brackets wok(\"No brackets:\", a) wok(\"With brackets:\", b) } Even when it is not required, many programmers add extra brackets to complicated expressions — not because they are needed, but so others (or themselves six months later) can understand the order right away without memorising a precedence table. What is 6 + 3 × 4? What is (6 + 3) × 4? 20 ÷ 5 × 2 equals... The order of operations is safe now. Next we look deeper at negative numbers — when a product or quotient turns negative, and the sign rules that often get mixed up."}, {"j": "Negative Numbers", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch5.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Negative numbers are not just \"numbers taken away\". They have their own sign rules when multiplied or divided — rules that are often remembered wrong when only memorised, not understood.", "t": "Sign rules for multiplying &amp; dividing The easiest way to remember: same signs → positive, different signs → negative. The same rule holds for division. Negative is the \"opposite/enemy\" of positive. Opposing once (multiplying by a negative) turns something into its opposite. Opposing twice (negative × negative) turns it back into a friend — that is why the result is positive. Absolute value (distance from zero) The absolute value, written |x| , is a number's distance from zero — and a distance is always positive (or zero), never negative. Reading −7 &lt; |−7| as \"−7 is smaller than itself\". But |−7|=7 , so what is compared is −7 &lt; 7 — which is true, not strange. The absolute value turns a number into another (positive) number; it does not just \"rub out the minus in place\". The math::abs() function Almost every programming language has a built-in absolute-value function, because the need for \"how far, regardless of direction\" is very common — for example the difference between two temperatures, the distance between points, or the error in a measurement. pub fn main() { let tempJakarta = 30 let tempPeak = -5 let gap = math::abs(tempJakarta - tempPeak) wok(\"Temperature gap:\", gap, \"degrees\") let product = (-4) * (-3) wok(\"(-4) * (-3) =\", product) } A bank balance can be negative (debt/credit), the depth of the sea is written as negative from sea level, and scores can drop below zero in many games. Every time you see a \"−\" in front of a number in an app, the rules of this c"}, {"j": "Fractions & Decimals", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch6.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Fractions and decimals are two different \"languages\" for the same thing: part of a whole. Knowing how to move between them makes many problems much more flexible.", "t": "The anatomy of a fraction The fraction 3 4 is read \"three quarters\" — something split into 4 equal parts (the denominator), of which we take 3 (the numerator). The fraction 8 12 can be simplified by dividing the numerator &amp; denominator by their GCD (the GCD of 8 and 12 is 4) → giving 2 3 . The GCD is covered in more detail in Chapter 10. From fraction to decimal, and back Adding fractions with different denominators This is the part most often done wrong: fractions can ONLY be added directly when their denominators are the same . If they differ, make the denominators equal first (using the LCM). Adding the numerators and the denominators directly: 1/2 + 1/3 ≠ 2/5 . This is WRONG — a fraction is not an ordinary pair of numbers; it stands for a single value whose base (the denominator) has to be made equal before combining. Rounding decimals A computer stores a float with limited precision, so the result of a division often needs rounding to display neatly. JRG provides math::round() , math::floor() (always down), and math::ceil() (always up). pub fn main() { let fraction = 5.0 / 8.0 wok(\"5/8 as a decimal:\", fraction) wok(\"round:\", math::round(0.625), \"→ as int:\", math::round(0.625) as int) wok(\"floor:\", math::floor(0.625) as int) wok(\"ceil:\", math::ceil(0.625) as int) } Try 0.1 + 0.2 in many programming languages — the result is often 0.30000000000000004 , not a tidy 0.3 ! That is because decimals are stored in binary, and not every decimal fraction can be represented exac"}, {"j": "Percentages (%)", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch7.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "\"Percent\" comes from the Latin per centum, meaning \"per hundred\". Once you remember that, percentages stop being a scary topic and become just a fraction over 100.", "t": "Three faces of one number 25%, 0.25, and 1/4 are exactly the same number , just written in three different forms. \"X% of N\" = (X ÷ 100) × N. For example, 20% of 150 = (20÷100)×150 = 0.2×150 = 30 . Discounts &amp; taxes (used most often) Percentage increase/decrease \"Up 50%, then down 50%\" does NOT bring you back to where you started! $100 up 50% → $150. $150 down 50% → $75, not $100. The reason: the second percentage is taken from a different base (from 150, not from 100 again). An automatic discount calculator fn priceAfterDiscount(price: float, discountPercent: float) -> float { return price * (1.0 - discountPercent / 100.0) } pub fn main() { let price = 150.0 let result = priceAfterDiscount(price, 20.0) wok(\"Final price: $\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", result)) } Because the formula \"price × (1 − discount/100)\" will be used again and again for many products. Writing it once as the function priceAfterDiscount() means no copy-pasting the same formula dozens of times — you just call the function. 30% of 90 is... An $80 shirt has a 25% discount. The final price? A price goes up 50%, then down 50%. Compared with the starting price, the result is? So far every number has had a known value. The next chapter introduces variables — letters that stand for a number that is not (or deliberately not yet) known, and how to find it with an equation."}, {"j": "Variables & Equations", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch8.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "A variable is an \"empty box\" standing for a number that is not known yet. An equation is a puzzle that gives you enough information to find what is in the box.", "t": "What is a variable? In x + 5 = 12 , the letter x is a variable — an empty slot whose value is not known yet, but can be found because there is a rule (the equation) linking it to other numbers. Whatever you do to one side of an equation (add, subtract, multiply, divide), you must do exactly the same to the other side. That keeps the \"scales\" balanced. Solving an equation step by step Moving a number to the other side without changing its sign. 3x + 7 = 22 does not become 3x = 22 + 7 — the right one is 3x = 22 − 7 , because the \"+7\" that moves becomes \"−7\" (the same as subtracting 7 from both sides). Bonus: two equations, two variables Sometimes one equation is not enough because two things are unknown. The way out: find two equations, then combine them by substitution . A problem like \"2 pencils + 1 book cost $13, and 1 pencil + 2 books cost $11; what does each cost?\" is a system of two equations. Very common in secondary-school word problems. let , mut , and const — three kinds of \"box\" JRG has three ways to declare a value, rather like three kinds of math variable: fixed, changeable, and truly locked. package main const TAX: float = 0.11 // never changes anywhere in the program pub fn main() { let x = 5 // fixed, cannot be changed again let mut score = 0 // may change score += 10 wok(\"x stays:\", x) wok(\"score changed:\", score) wok(\"tax is constant:\", TAX) } When a program \"solves\" 3x+7=22 , it really runs the same algebra steps a person would: let x = (22 - 7) / 3 . The com"}, {"j": "Math Functions", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch9.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "A function is a consistent \"machine\": put something in, get a predictable result. The f(x) notation is just a formal way of writing that machine.", "t": "f(x) = an input-output machine f(x) = 2x + 3 is read: \"the function f, with input x, gives 2 times x plus 3.\" f(x) is NOT f times x — f is the name of the machine, x is what goes into it. Reading \"f(x)\" as the product \"f times x\". But f is the function's name (like a variable name for a machine), not a number being multiplied. Compare it with sin(x) — nobody reads that as \"sin times x\". Domain &amp; range — the machine's limits The domain = every input that may go into the machine. The range = every possible result that can come out. For example: for f(x) = 1/x , the domain is every real number except 0 — because dividing by zero is undefined (remember Chapter 3!). A function in code = a function in math This is the most direct meeting point in all of the material: fn f(x: float) -> float in JRG literally is f(x) in math. Exactly the same idea — the input goes in, the rule runs, the output comes out. fn f(x: float) -> float { return 2.0 * x + 3.0 } pub fn main() { wok(\"f(4) =\", f(4.0)) wok(\"f(0) =\", f(0.0)) wok(\"f(-1) =\", f(-1.0)) } Every time you call a function in a program — priceAfterDiscount() in Chapter 7, isEven() in Chapter 3 — you are already using the idea of f(x) without noticing. Functional programming is basically math run by a computer. If f(x) = 3x − 2, what is f(5)? f(x) is read as... For f(x) = 1/x, which value of x is NOT in the domain? We move to applied topics: prime numbers, the GCD, and the LCM — ideas that come back in fractions (already used in Chapter"}, {"j": "Primes, GCD & LCM", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch10.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Prime numbers are the \"atoms\" of all integers — every other number can be broken down into a combination of primes. The GCD and the LCM come from there.", "t": "Prime numbers A prime number can only be divided evenly by 1 and itself. 1 is not prime (by mathematical agreement), and 2 is the only even prime. You only need to try dividing by the primes from 2 up to the square root of the number. If none divides evenly, it is prime. To check 97: just try 2, 3, 5, 7 (because √97≈9.8) — no need to go up to 96! Prime factorisation → GCD &amp; LCM Mixing up the GCD and the LCM. Remember: the GCD is always ≤ both numbers (it looks for a shared factor, so it is smaller), while the LCM is always ≥ both numbers (it looks for a shared multiple, so it is bigger or equal). Euclid's algorithm for the GCD Breaking numbers into prime factors is slow for big numbers. There is a much faster way, over 2000 years old: Euclid's algorithm — GCD(a,b) = GCD(b, a mod b), repeated until the remainder is zero. fn gcd(a: int, b: int) -> int { let mut x = a let mut y = b while y != 0 { let rest = x % y x = y y = rest } return x } fn lcm(a: int, b: int) -> int { return (a * b) / gcd(a, b) } pub fn main() { wok(\"GCD(18,30) =\", gcd(18, 30)) wok(\"LCM(18,30) =\", lcm(18, 30)) } RSA encryption, which secures online payments, relies on the fact that multiplying two large primes is easy, but taking the result apart again (factorisation) is very hard for a computer — even for numbers hundreds of digits long. Which one is a prime number? The GCD of 12 and 18 is... Which statement is true? Now we turn to basic statistics — how to sum up a set of data in a few key numbers that"}, {"j": "Basic Statistics", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch11.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Statistics is the art of summing up a mountain of data in a handful of numbers that still mean something. Four key numbers: the mean, the median, the mode, and the range.", "t": "Example data: test scores of 7 students The mean (average) The median (middle value) Looking for the median WITHOUT sorting the data first. The median is \"the middle value after sorting\" — take the middle of the original (unsorted) order and the result is almost certainly wrong. Mode &amp; range Mean vs median: which to trust? The mean is very sensitive to extreme values (outliers); the median is not. Imagine the salaries of 5 employees: [4k, 4.5k, 5k, 5k, 50k] (the boss is included). If the data has extreme outliers (salaries, house prices, wealth), the median usually describes \"the typical\" more honestly. The mean suits fairly even data such as test scores or heights. Bonus: how \"spread out\" is the data? Two classes can have the same mean but very different spreads. The standard deviation measures this — on average, \"how far\" each value is from the mean. Computing the mean of an array fn mean(data: []float) -> float { let mut total = 0.0 for _, value in data { total += value } return total / data.len() as float } pub fn main() { let scores = [70.0, 85.0, 90.0, 65.0, 85.0, 100.0, 60.0] wok(\"Mean:\", mean(scores)) wok(\"Highest score:\", arr::max(scores)) wok(\"Lowest score:\", arr::min(scores)) wok(\"Median:\", arr::median(scores)) } The mean() function above is the heart of the report-card calculator project you will build yourself later. This module is not just theory — you will use it directly. Data: [3, 7, 3, 9]. What is the mode? When is the median more trustworthy than the me"}, {"j": "Plane Geometry", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch12.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "The perimeter measures the \"distance around\" a shape. The area measures \"how much space\" it covers. Two things that often get swapped, even though they mean very different things.", "t": "Perimeter &amp; area formulas for flat shapes Using the diameter when the formula needs the radius (r = d÷2). A circle with a diameter of 10 has r=5, not r=10 — this mistake makes the area 4× bigger than it should be. Area units MUST be squared (cm², m²), while a perimeter keeps ordinary units (cm, m). Writing \"area = 20 cm\" (with no square) is a very common mistake — and it really shows a confused idea, not just a typo. A combined example Area functions for several shapes fn circleArea(r: float) -> float { return math::PI * r * r } fn triangleArea(base: float, height: float) -> float { return 0.5 * base * height } pub fn main() { let diameter = 14.0 let radius = diameter / 2.0 wok(\"Circle area:\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", circleArea(radius))) wok(\"Triangle area:\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", triangleArea(10.0, 6.0))) } Every 2D game uses plane geometry for collision detection — checking whether two boxes or circles touch uses exactly the formulas you learn in this chapter. The area of a square with 9cm sides is... A circle has a diameter of 20cm; its radius is... The right unit for AREA is... Next: exponents (powers) and roots — including how bank interest grows exponentially, not linearly."}, {"j": "Exponents & Roots", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch13.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "An exponent is a short way of writing repeated multiplication. A root is its opposite. Together they explain why money in the bank (and viruses, and populations) can grow much faster than intuition expects.", "t": "The rules of powers Thinking (a+b)² = a²+b² . This is WRONG! The right one: (a+b)² = a²+2ab+b² . Try it with numbers: (2+3)²=25, but 2²+3²=4+9=13. Clearly different. Roots — the opposite of powers Exponential growth: compound interest This is the clearest example of how \"small at the start, huge at the end\" is the nature of exponents. An exponential graph is flat at the start, then shoots up. The human brain tends to think linearly, so exponential growth (bank interest, the spread of a virus, going viral on social media) often feels surprising — even though that is simply its nature. Simulating compound interest pub fn main() { let principal = 1000.0 let interest = 0.1 let years = 20.0 let result = principal * math::pow(1.0 + interest, years) wok(\"After 20 years: $\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", result)) wok(\"√144 =\", math::sqrt(144.0)) } A power with a decimal or variable exponent (not a small whole number) needs a special mathematical algorithm, not just repeated multiplication — that is why there is a separate math::pow() function. 2⁵ equals... (3+4)² equals... What is 5⁰? If an exponent answers \"what is the result of this power\", a logarithm answers the opposite: \"what power do I need to get this result?\""}, {"j": "Logarithms", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch14.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "If an exponent asks \"what is 2 to the power 5?\", a logarithm asks the opposite: \"what power must 2 be raised to, to get 32?\" The answer: 5. That is log₂(32) = 5.", "t": "A logarithm = the reverse question of a power log(x) with no base usually means base 10 (the common log). ln(x) means base e ≈2.718 (the natural log, used in calculus &amp; science). In computer science, log₂(x) shows up most often. Three log rules to remember log(a+b) ≠ log(a)+log(b) — the addition rule ONLY applies to multiplication inside the log, not to addition inside the log. There is no simple formula for the log of a sum. Why does log₂ matter in computer science? Log₂ answers \"how many times can we halve before nothing is left\" — and that is exactly what happens in binary search , a method far faster than checking items one by one. Computing logs in JRG pub fn main() { wok(\"log10(1000) =\", math::log10(1000.0)) wok(\"log2(1024) =\", math::log2(1024.0)) // Estimated binary-search steps for n items let n = 1000000.0 let steps = math::ceil(math::log2(n)) wok(\"Estimated binary-search steps:\", steps) } Decibels (loudness), the Richter scale (earthquake strength), and pH (acidity) are all logarithmic. A magnitude-6 earthquake is not \"2× stronger\" than a magnitude 3 — it is about 1000× stronger, because the scale is logarithmic. log₂(16) = ? log(6) + log(2) equals... Why is log₂ used so often in computer science? Next: probability — how \"likely\" an event is, and how to compute it exactly (not just a guess)."}, {"j": "Probability", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch15.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Probability turns the word \"maybe\" into an exact number — between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain). In between, every uncertainty in life gets a number.", "t": "The basic probability formula A probability is always between 0 (not possible at all) and 1 (certain to happen). 50% = 0.5 means \"equally likely to happen and not to happen.\" The complement rule AND vs OR — two formulas that get swapped After a coin lands \"Heads\" 5 times in a row, many people are sure \"Tails\" is now more likely on the next throw. This is WRONG — every new throw is still 50-50, whatever happened before. A coin has no \"memory\". Computing a combined probability fn dieProbability(wantedSides: float) -> float { return wantedSides / 6.0 } pub fn main() { let pOneDie = dieProbability(1.0) wok(\"P(rolling a 6) =\", pOneDie) // AND → multiply: two dice both 6 let pTwoDice = pOneDie * pOneDie wok(\"P(two dice both 6) =\", pTwoDice) // The complement wok(\"P(NOT rolling a 6) =\", 1.0 - pOneDie) } Insurance companies, casinos, recommendation algorithms, and weather models all run on probability. Every time a weather app says \"70% chance of rain\", that is a probability computed in ways like this chapter. P(rolling an even number on a die) is... A coin is thrown 4× and lands Heads every time. The chance the 5th throw is Heads is... The word \"AND\" in a probability problem usually means... The last four chapters before the final project widen your view: ratios &amp; proportions, 3D solids, sequences &amp; series, and basic trigonometry."}, {"j": "Ratios & Proportions", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch16.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "A ratio compares two things relatively, not absolutely. \"2 cups of flour : 1 cup of sugar\" holds whether you bake 1 tray or 100 trays of cake — that is the power of a ratio.", "t": "Notation &amp; simplifying ratios The ratio 8:12 is written the same way as a fraction, and simplified the same way — divide by the GCD. Direct proportion When one value goes up, the other goes up by the same factor. A recipe for 4 people has to be scaled for 10 — every ingredient grows by the same ratio. Inverse proportion Sometimes the link is inverse : the more workers, the less time is needed (assuming everyone works equally). Using the direct-proportion formula (multiplying straight across) on a case that is really inverse. The sign of an inverse problem: the bigger one gets, the smaller the other (workers vs time, speed vs travel time). Always ask: if one goes up, should the other go up or down? Map &amp; model scales A recipe-scaling function fn scaleRecipe(originalAmount: float, originalServings: float, newServings: float) -> float { let perServing = originalAmount / originalServings return perServing * newServings } fn inverseTime(originalWorkers: float, originalDays: float, newWorkers: float) -> float { let totalWork = originalWorkers * originalDays return totalWork / newWorkers } pub fn main() { wok(\"Flour for 10 people:\", str::format(\"{:.0}\", scaleRecipe(200.0, 4.0, 10.0)), \"g\") wok(\"Days for 4 workers:\", str::format(\"{:.0}\", inverseTime(6.0, 8.0, 4.0)), \"days\") } Screen aspect ratios (16:9, 4:3), image/CSS scaling ( object-fit ), colour mixing (RGB), and API rate-limit budgets are all about exactly the ratios and proportions you just learned. The ratio 15:25 simp"}, {"j": "Solids", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch17.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Plane geometry (Chapter 12) measures flat surfaces. Solids add one more dimension: depth. Now we compute what is inside (the volume), not just the area.", "t": "Volume &amp; surface-area formulas Volume units MUST be cubed (cm³, m³) — not cm² (that is area) or cm (that is length). Forgetting to raise the unit's power is a sign the idea of \"dimensions\" is not really understood yet, not just a slip of the pen. An example: a water tank A cube vs a sphere of equal volume — which has the smaller surface? An interesting fact: of all shapes with the same volume, the sphere has the smallest surface area . That is why water droplets, soap bubbles, and planets tend to be round — nature \"saves\" surface automatically. Computing the volume of several solids fn cuboidVolume(l: float, w: float, h: float) -> float { return l * w * h } fn cylinderVolume(r: float, h: float) -> float { return math::PI * r * r * h } fn sphereVolume(r: float) -> float { return (4.0 / 3.0) * math::PI * math::pow(r, 3.0) } pub fn main() { wok(\"Water tank volume:\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", cuboidVolume(2.0, 1.5, 1.0)), \"m3\") wok(\"Cylinder volume:\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", cylinderVolume(3.0, 10.0)), \"cm3\") wok(\"Sphere volume r=6:\", str::format(\"{:.2}\", sphereVolume(6.0)), \"cm3\") } 3D graphics in games and films build every object from these basic shapes (or more complicated versions of them). Computing volume also matters for packaging, shipping (volumetric weight), and the physics engines in games. The volume of a cube with 4cm sides is... Which unit is right for VOLUME? A cuboid measures 3×4×5 cm. What is its volume? Now on to sequences &amp; series — number patterns in order, a"}, {"j": "Sequences & Series", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch18.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "A sequence is a pattern of numbers in order. Once you find the pattern, you can guess the 100th term without writing the 99 before it — and add them up without adding one by one.", "t": "Arithmetic sequences — a fixed difference Each term comes from the one before by adding the same number (called the common difference , d). Geometric sequences — a fixed ratio Each term comes from the one before by multiplying by the same number (called the common ratio , r). Treating every sequence as arithmetic. Check first: is the DIFFERENCE between terms fixed (arithmetic), or the QUOTIENT between terms fixed (geometric)? 2,4,8,16 is not arithmetic (the differences are 2,4,8 — not fixed), but it is geometric (a fixed ratio of 2). Series — the sum of all the terms The young mathematician Gauss was asked to add up 1 to 100. He answered 5050 at once — not by counting one by one, but by pairing (1+100), (2+99), (3+98)... which each add up to 101, 50 pairs in all: 50×101=5050. That is the heart of the arithmetic-series formula. Generating a sequence with a loop pub fn main() { wok(\"Arithmetic sequence (a=2, d=3):\") let a = 2 let d = 3 for _, n in 1..=6 { let term = a + (n - 1) * d wok_(term, \" \") } wok(\"\") // Series: the total of the first 6 terms let mut total = 0 for _, n in 1..=6 { total += a + (n - 1) * d } wok(\"Sum of the first 6 terms:\", total) } The loop for _, n in 1..=6 basically \"generates\" the sequence term by term — exactly the mechanism the Un formula above describes, just run automatically by the computer. The sequence 4, 9, 14, 19, ... What is the 7th term? Which one is a GEOMETRIC sequence? The sum 1+2+3+...+50 (Gauss's formula) is... The last theory chapter: b"}, {"j": "Basic Trigonometry", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch19.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Trigonometry answers the question: if we know one angle and one side of a right triangle, can we work out all the other sides & angles? The answer is yes — through sin, cos, and tan.", "t": "The three sides of a right triangle Relative to the angle we are looking at (θ): SOH-CAH-TOA — the mnemonic to remember Sin=Opposite/Hypotenuse, Cos=Adjacent/Hypotenuse, Tan=Opposite/Adjacent. Read the first letter of each word: S-O-H, C-A-H, T-O-A. An example: measuring a height without climbing Swapping the \"opposite\" and \"adjacent\" sides — both are relative to the angle being used , not fixed positions in the picture. If the known angle moves to another angle of the same triangle, which side is \"opposite\" and which \"adjacent\" changes too! Special angles Trigonometric functions — watch out for radians! The functions math::sin/cos/tan in JRG (as in most programming languages) take angles in radians , not degrees. The conversion: radians = degrees × (π/180). fn toRadians(degrees: float) -> float { return degrees * (math::PI / 180.0) } pub fn main() { let angle = toRadians(60.0) let distance = 20.0 let height = distance * math::tan(angle) wok(\"Pole height:\", height, \"metres\") } Calling math::sin(60.0) directly without converting to radians — the result is COMPLETELY WRONG, because 60 is taken as 60 radians (more than 9 full turns!), not 60°. This is one of the most common trigonometry bugs in every programming language. sin θ is defined as... tan θ is defined as... Before calling math::sin() in JRG, an angle in degrees must be... All 19 chapters are done! Time to bring it all together — especially statistics — into one real project: an interactive report-card calculator."}, {"j": "Project: Report-Card Calculator", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/ch20.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Where the whole journey meets. This project combines statistics (Chapter 11), functions (Chapter 9), and variables (Chapter 8) into one genuinely useful tool: an automatic report-card calculator.", "t": "What will be computed? Put in a few test scores. The tool computes the average (mean) , the median , the highest &amp; lowest scores, then decides a letter grade from the average — every idea you mastered one by one in the earlier chapters, now working together. The report-card calculator in JRG This program uses ideas from Chapters 8, 9, and 11 at once: functions, loops, the mean, and the median. fn mean(data: []float) -> float { let mut total = 0.0 for _, n in data { total += n } return total / data.len() as float } fn grade(average: float) -> string { if average >= 90.0 { return \"A - Outstanding\" } else if average >= 80.0 { return \"B - Good\" } else if average >= 70.0 { return \"C - Fair\" } else if average >= 60.0 { return \"D - Needs Work\" } else { return \"E - Not Passed Yet\" } } pub fn main() { let scores = [78.0, 85.0, 92.0, 67.0, 88.0] let average = mean(scores) wok(\"Average:\", average) wok(\"Median:\", arr::median(scores)) wok(\"Grade:\", grade(average)) } Press Edit , replace the contents of scores with your own report-card scores, then press Run . When you are done, mark this project as finished with the button below. From getting to know numbers in Chapter 1 to computing grades automatically here, you have been through 20 full chapters — arithmetic, algebra, statistics, 2D &amp; 3D geometry, exponents, logarithms, probability, sequences, and trigonometry. More importantly: you have seen every idea twice — as math and as code that really runs. Keep that habit — math and pr"}, {"j": "Reference & Glossary", "u": "matematika_x_jrg/referensi.html", "g": "Math × JRG", "k": "Every formula from the 20 chapters, summed up on one page. Save it (Ctrl+F / Cmd+F and type a keyword) or print it before a test.", "t": "Basic Arithmetic (Chapters 1–5) Further Concepts (Chapters 6–9) Applied Math (Chapters 10–15) Advanced Level (Chapters 16–19) JRG Syntax — Summary The Most Common Traps This page teaches the basics. For the full story of the whole language — the standard library, FFI, its four engines, and how to install it — open the official documentation at jrg-lang.org , and the source code at github.com/Kodok-Kejepit/jrg . Glossary JRG Calling Other Languages (FFI) Code someone else has already written does not need rewriting. JRG can call it directly &mdash; which is what makes it usable for real work, not just practice. Kotlin is the main language for Android apps, and its ecosystem is large. Because Kotlin runs on the JVM, JRG calls it the same way as Java &mdash; what differs is only the NAMES its compiler uses: functions in a file Calc.kt land in a class CalcKt , and an object becomes a field named INSTANCE . Besides FFI, JRG has dok:: &mdash; more than a hundred functions for building applications: an HTTP router, WebSocket, SQLite, templates that escape HTML themselves, signed cookies, and password hashing. A complete example is in the repo: examples/web_app_demo.jrg . Use the search in the sidebar to jump straight to the chapter that covers a formula in full with examples — this page is deliberately short, as a reminder, not a replacement for reading the chapters."}];
